{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat control-reader-brief.md third-points.json controls/control-packet-{1,2,4,6,7,9}.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat control-reader-brief.md third-points.json controls/control-packet-{1,2,4,6,7,9}.json'","aggregated_output":"# Stage B: reading the control theses (read-only)\n\nYou are an independent reader. Do not edit files, run model calls or start runs, and do not read any file other than this brief\nand the one packet you are given.\n\nEach item of your packet pairs a **ruler question** (a question written by hand for an earlier, frozen measurement, with its\nunit, due day and ladder of rungs) with a **judged question** the system wrote itself for a thesis it opened (its question, the\noutcome it looks at, due day, year and rungs). It also lists **reader B's decisive items**: the evidence an earlier reader, called\nreader B, cited for the frozen answer to the ruler question (each with its day, title, passage and meaning), and **the evidence\nshown to the judge**: every evidence entry the system's judge was shown for the judged question in any of its requests on or\nbefore 2026-07-14 (empty when there is none). You are not told either answer, and you do not judge which answer is right.\n\nFor each item, answer three questions.\n\n1. `same_question`: **yes** when the judged question asks what the ruler question asks: the same quantity measured the same way\n   (the same total, scope and unit: not a part of it, a ceiling on it, another quantity or another unit), or the same event,\n   for the same period. **no** otherwise. Wording may differ; a judged question that asks for more or less than the ruler's,\n   or for another period, is **no**.\n2. `same_due`: for an **event** (a yes-or-no question), **yes** only when the judged question's deadline is the ruler's\n   deadline. For a **figure**, **yes** when the two due days both mark the publication of the same period's figure (for\n   example, both the annual report of the same fiscal year, even if the two days differ); **no** otherwise.\n3. `items`: for **each** of reader B's decisive items, by its id, **shown** when its content is present in the evidence shown to\n   the judge (the same fact or figure, in any wording, from any source), **not_shown** when it is absent. Search the entries for\n   the figure or the fact; do not mark an item shown because a related item is.\n\nReturn one JSON object per item, one per line, in packet order, and nothing else (no prose, no code fence):\n`{\"control\": 3, \"same_question\": \"yes\"|\"no\", \"same_due\": \"yes\"|\"no\", \"items\": {\"i005\": \"shown\"|\"not_shown\", ...}, \"why\": \"one sentence naming what decides each no and each not_shown\"}`.\n\n## Third reader (only where the two readers differ)\n\nA third reader of each family is given this brief, the packet, and a list of the disputed points, each a control number and a\nfield (`same_question`, `same_due`, or `item <id>`), without the two readers' answers. For each point, answer that one question\nafresh by the rules above and return one JSON object per point, one per line, and nothing else:\n`{\"control\": 3, \"field\": \"item i005\", \"ruling\": \"yes\"|\"no\"|\"shown\"|\"not_shown\", \"why\": \"one sentence\"}` (`yes`/`no` for the two\nquestion fields, `shown`/`not_shown` for an item).\n[\n {\n  \"control\": 1,\n  \"field\": \"same_question\"\n },\n {\n  \"control\": 4,\n  \"field\": \"same_question\"\n },\n {\n  \"control\": 5,\n  \"field\": \"same_question\"\n },\n {\n  \"control\": 8,\n  \"field\": \"same_question\"\n },\n {\n  \"control\": 12,\n  \"field\": \"item i256\"\n },\n {\n  \"control\": 13,\n  \"field\": \"same_question\"\n },\n {\n  \"control\": 13,\n  \"field\": \"item i256\"\n },\n {\n  \"control\": 15,\n  \"field\": \"item i309\"\n },\n {\n  \"control\": 16,\n  \"field\": \"same_question\"\n },\n {\n  \"control\": 22,\n  \"field\": \"item i309\"\n }\n]\n[\n {\n  \"control\": 1,\n  \"ruler_question\": {\n   \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a guarantee of OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease by 2027-06-30?\",\n   \"unit\": \"yes/no\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-06-30\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Yes\",\n     \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has signed the guarantee by the due day.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"No\",\n     \"condition\": \"It has not.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"judged_question\": {\n   \"view_id\": \"v3\",\n   \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee for OpenAI's lease of the large Ohio data center by December 31, 2027?\",\n   \"looks_at\": \"NVIDIA's reported negotiations to guarantee OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-12-31\",\n   \"year\": \"2027\",\n   \"kind\": \"asset\"\n  },\n  \"judged_rungs\": [\n   {\n    \"position\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"Yes\",\n    \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has signed a financial guarantee for OpenAI's lease of the Ohio data center by December 31, 2027.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"No\",\n    \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has not signed a financial guarantee for OpenAI's lease of the Ohio data center by December 31, 2027.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i005\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-01\",\n    \"title\": \"NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.\",\n    \"passage\": \"NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA (per The Information)\",\n     \"what\": \"negotiating to guarantee OpenAI's lease of a large Ohio data center\",\n     \"how_much\": \"the data center could cost as much as $500 billion if fully built (asset cost, not the guarantee amount)\",\n     \"condition\": \"in negotiation; nothing signed\",\n     \"status\": \"rumour\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i006\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-01\",\n    \"title\": \"NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.\",\n    \"passage\": \"NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"what\": \"guarantees of customer data center leases, in exchange for rights to buy their shares\",\n     \"how_much\": \"an additional $3.5 billion\",\n     \"period\": \"quarter ended April 2026 (filed May)\",\n     \"status\": \"happened\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i116\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n    \"passage\": \"Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n     \"what\": \"NVIDIA backstops datacenter leases for neoclouds (a second kind of obligation)\",\n     \"status\": \"happened\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i119\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n    \"passage\": \"Nvidia’s backstop program is only getting started, and we see announcements coming faster every quarter. Many Neoclouds are already deep in negotiations with Nvidia\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n     \"what\": \"more NVIDIA backstops exist but are not public; many neoclouds in negotiation; announcements accelerating\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\",\n     \"status_note\": \"existing undisclosed backstops; more deals are a forecast\"\n    }\n   }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\": [\n   \"- e6 [rumour; moomoo.com; original source The Information ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] NVIDIA is reportedly negotiating to guarantee OpenAI's lease of a large Ohio data center; the facility could cost as much as $500 billion if fully built out at current prices.\\n    Quote: According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power, and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\"\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"control\": 2,\n  \"ruler_question\": {\n   \"question\": \"Will Nvidia begin shipping its Rubin generation by December 31, 2026?\",\n   \"unit\": \"yes/no\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2026-12-31\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Yes\",\n     \"condition\": \"Nvidia has begun shipping Rubin by December 31, 2026.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"No\",\n     \"condition\": \"Nvidia has not begun shipping Rubin by December 31, 2026.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": null\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"judged_question\": {\n   \"view_id\": \"v4\",\n   \"question\": \"Will Nvidia begin shipping its Vera Rubin platform by December 31, 2026?\",\n   \"looks_at\": \"Whether Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform starts shipping in 2026.\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2026-12-31\",\n   \"year\": \"2026\",\n   \"kind\": \"asset\"\n  },\n  \"judged_rungs\": [\n   {\n    \"position\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"Yes\",\n    \"condition\": \"Nvidia has begun shipping Vera Rubin by December 31, 2026.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"No\",\n    \"condition\": \"Nvidia has not begun shipping Vera Rubin by December 31, 2026.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i105\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n    \"passage\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"what\": \"Vera Rubin systems in full production; shipping to eight cloud partners including AWS, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud\",\n     \"period\": \"this fall (2026)\",\n     \"status\": \"happened (full production); plan (shipping this fall)\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i219\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia Vera Rubin rack costs $7.8M — memory is why\",\n    \"passage\": \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"what\": \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 first shipments and volume ramp\",\n     \"period\": \"first shipments Q3 2026; volume ramp Q4 2026\",\n     \"status\": \"plan\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i202\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated]\",\n    \"passage\": \"Nvidia's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, aren't part of the reported delay.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"Tom's Hardware\",\n     \"what\": \"scope of the reported delay: Rubin Ultra/Kyber only; 2026 Rubin on Oberon unaffected\",\n     \"period\": \"2026\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i207\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia Delays Next-Generation Kyber Server Architecture as Manufacturing Hurdles Give Rivals a Rare Opening\",\n    \"passage\": \"the company’s current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and set to start shipping this fall to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA (as restated)\",\n     \"what\": \"Rubin systems in full production; first shipments to eight cloud partners incl. AWS, Azure, Google Cloud\",\n     \"period\": \"this fall\",\n     \"status\": \"happened\",\n     \"status_note\": \"in full production; shipping this fall is a plan\"\n    }\n   }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\": [\n   \"- e25 [third_party_view; Justin Pope; original source Justin Pope 2026-07-02; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] Motley Fool analyst Justin Pope said Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform was in full production and could begin shipping in 2026.\\n    Quote: Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year.\",\n   \"- e37 [fact; BGR.com, reporting on NVIDIA's blog post; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a8fab49de53d125314a6688a 2026-07-04] NVIDIA announced that its DSX AI factories are hosting Rubin AI infrastructure without fans or cold aisles.\\n    Quote: In Nvidia's blog post by Josh Parker, head of corporate sustainability at Nvidia, it was announced that Nvidia DSX AI factories are hosting Nvidia's Rubin AI infrastructure with no fans or cold aisles.\",\n   \"- e45 [fact; CNBC; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are scheduled to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including AWS, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\\n    Quote: Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\",\n   \"- e46 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] NVIDIA plans to launch its first stand-alone CPU this fall as part of the Vera Rubin platform.\\n    Quote: In fact, it's launching its first-ever stand-alone CPU this fall as part of the Vera Rubin platform.\",\n   \"- e45 [fact; CNBC; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are scheduled to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including AWS, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\\n    Quote: Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e86; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e47 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com, recounting SemiAnalysis's claims; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:68a3ed21f77b935d9fa7ec5e 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis said the Rubin Ultra high-density rack design had slipped by more than 12 months because of persistent manufacturing problems with its PCB midplane.\\n    Quote: SemiAnalysis claims the high-density rack design built for Rubin Ultra GPUs has slipped by more than 12 months. The firm blamed persistent manufacturing problems with the system's complex PCB midplane.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e89; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e48 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com, recounting SemiAnalysis's claim; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:68a3ed21f77b935d9fa7ec5e 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis claimed Nvidia scrapped its NVL72x2 back-to-back rack after hyperscaler customers pushed back.\\n    Quote: The firm also claimed Nvidia scrapped its NVL72x2 back-to-back rack after pushback from hyperscaler customers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e91, e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e49 [party_statement; NVIDIA, as reported by finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:68a3ed21f77b935d9fa7ec5e 2026-07-06] Nvidia told media outlets that its product roadmap remained intact, rejecting claims of delays and a scrapped rack design.\\n    Quote: Nvidia rejected the claims, telling media outlets that its roadmap remains intact.\",\n   \"- e62 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source MarketScale 2026-07-06; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack first shipments were scheduled for Q3 2026, with volume ramping in Q4 2026.\\n    Quote: First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\",\n   \"- e66 [third_party_view; 247WallSt author Danielle Liverance; original source 247WallSt author Danielle Liverance 2026-07-06; from gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06] Reports of delays across NVIDIA’s upcoming Vera Rubin server-system lineup put the product schedule at risk.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\",\n   \"- e73 [rumour; the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA canceled the upcoming four-compute-die version of its Rubin Ultra chip.\\n    Quote: The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e80; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e74 [rumour; the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's CPO-NVSwitch interconnect technology will not arrive until the Feynman generation, the generation after next.\\n    Quote: A key interconnect technology called CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman.\",\n   \"- e75 [third_party_view; the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis said NVIDIA currently lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems and that this could give AMD's MI500X and Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to compete.\\n    Quote: That means Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now. The gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e94; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e76 [rumour; the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA plans to sell more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor to make up for the shortfall.\\n    Quote: Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\",\n   \"- e80 [rumour; Investing.com, summarizing SemiAnalysis’s weekend report; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA had scrapped a four-die configuration for a downsized version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra platform.\\n    Quote: Additionally, the report pointed to a downsized version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra platform, claiming a crucial four-die configuration had been scrapped.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e73, e95; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e86 [third_party_view; tikr.com (analyst); original source tikr.com (analyst) 2026-07-06; from gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250 2026-07-06] Nvidia’s current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are set to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight major cloud partners, including AWS, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud.\\n    Quote: That’s likely because the company’s current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and set to start shipping this fall to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e45; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e89 [rumour; Tom’s Hardware, reporting SemiAnalysis’s thread on X; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack, intended for Rubin Ultra GPUs, is delayed until 2028, more than 12 months later than planned.\\n    Quote: Nvidia reportedly won't ship its Kyber NVL144 rack until 2028, a delay of more than 12 months that pushes the cabinet meant for 2027's Rubin Ultra GPUs into the following year, according to a SemiAnalysis thread on X.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e47; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e90 [rumour; Tom’s Hardware, reporting SemiAnalysis’s thread on X; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis attributed the reported Kyber NVL144 delay to manufacturing challenges with its PCB orthogonal backplane.\\n    Quote: The holdup is ostensibly being caused by manufacturing challenges with a PCB midplane that connects eight Oberon racks between the NVSwitches, which Nvidia calls the orthogonal backplane.\",\n   \"- e91 [rumour; Tom’s Hardware, reporting SemiAnalysis’s thread on X; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA cancelled the NVL72x2 stopgap rack and that no proven alternative is available to expand Rubin Ultra scale-up in 2027.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is also understood to have killed NVL72x2, a stopgap rack designed to tide customers over, and that no proven alternative is now available to widen Rubin Ultra's scale-up in 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e48; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e92 [rumour; Tom’s Hardware, reporting SemiAnalysis’s thread on X; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis said NVIDIA abandoned the NVL72x2 stopgap after its largest customers balked at operating two linked cabinets as one unit.\\n    Quote: NVL72x2 would have bolted two Oberon racks back-to-back to reach Kyber-class density over copper NVLink, per SemiAnalysis, which said Nvidia abandoned the stopgap after its largest customers balked at running two linked cabinets as a single unit.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e48; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e93 [rumour; Tom’s Hardware, reporting SemiAnalysis’s thread on X; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] Tom’s Hardware reported SemiAnalysis’s view that NVIDIA’s NVL576 configuration may be delayed or ship in low volume until co-packaged optics mature.\\n    Quote: NVL576, a separate configuration tying eight racks together through co-packaged optics, is likely to slip too or ship in low volume until that optical technology matures.\",\n   \"- e94 [third_party_view; Tom’s Hardware, reporting SemiAnalysis’s thread on X; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] Tom’s Hardware reported that SemiAnalysis said NVIDIA has no proven way to expand Rubin Ultra scale-up beyond Oberon’s level in 2027.\\n    Quote: These cancellations leave Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" meaning the largest single Rubin Ultra domain in 2027 could match, but not exceed, what Oberon already delivers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e75; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e95 [rumour; Tom’s Hardware, reporting SemiAnalysis’s thread on X; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA changed Rubin Ultra from a quad-chiplet GPU to a dual-chiplet part the week before publication, halving per-package compute.\\n    Quote: Nvidia dropped the quad-chiplet Rubin Ultra GPU for a dual-chiplet part last week over manufacturing execution concerns, halving the accelerator's per-package compute.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e80; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e102 [party_statement; fool.com author; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:9026c944c63575a080ce7ba9 2026-07-07] Nvidia announced plans to launch its Vera Rubin Space-1 Module, with no launch date specified.\\n    Quote: Also, Nvidia announced in March that it will be launching its Vera Rubin Space-1 Module at some point in the future.\",\n   \"- e109 [third_party_view; The Straits Times; original source The Straits Times 2026-07-08; from gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c 2026-07-08] Nvidia is working to release its Rubin processor lineup in the second half of 2026.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is now working to release an even more powerful line-up called Rubin in the second half of 2026 .\",\n   \"- e117 [party_statement; Futuriom, quoting an NVIDIA spokesperson; original source NVIDIA spokesperson ; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] An NVIDIA spokesperson said the company's roadmap was intact and Vera Rubin Ultra remained on track to scale to an NVL576 domain in 2027.\\n    Quote: An NVIDIA spokesperson said, “Our road map is intact.” Further, \\\"Vera Rubin Ultra will scale up to a larger NVL576 domain in its planned 2027 timeline.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e89 (); both are in force: SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack, intended for Rubin Ultra GPUs, is delayed until 2028, more than 12 months later than planned.\",\n   \"- e241 [fact; en.sedaily.com; original source LG Electronics ; from gnews:bf96def37a9c3a5f19f5d43c 2026-07-12] LG Electronics is developing an AI-chip server rack targeting Nvidia and plans to begin mass production in 2027.\\n    Quote: LG Electronics (066570.KS) is developing an AI chip-based server rack targeting Nvidia and plans to begin mass production next year.\",\n   \"- e242 [fact; en.sedaily.com; original source LG Electronics ; from gnews:bf96def37a9c3a5f19f5d43c 2026-07-12] LG Electronics completed a prototype of an AI server rack matching Nvidia Vera Rubin's compute-tray specifications in the first half of 2026; it plans reliability evaluations in the second half, to seek orders from global big tech companies afterward, and to begin mass production in 2027.\\n    Quote: According to the semiconductor industry on the 12th, LG Electronics' Production Research Institute (PRI) completed development of an AI server rack prototype matching the compute tray specifications of Nvidia's \\\"Vera Rubin\\\" in the first half of the year. LG plans to secure orders from global big tech companies after reliability evaluations in the second half and begin mass production next year.\",\n   \"- e254 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:dedacecd6d2ed5192c307199 2026-07-13] Nvidia said development of its next-generation Rubin Ultra platform remains on schedule.\\n    Quote: The company said development of its next-generation Rubin Ultra platform remains on schedule\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e66 (2026-07-06); both are in force: Reports of delays across NVIDIA’s upcoming Vera Rubin server-system lineup put the product schedule at risk.\",\n   \"- e255 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:dedacecd6d2ed5192c307199 2026-07-13] Nvidia said there have been no changes to its NVLink architecture.\\n    Quote: confirmed there have been no changes to its NVLink architecture.\",\n   \"- e354 [third_party_view; EBC; original source EBC ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] EBC reported that Vera Rubin remained officially on track for late-2026 shipping.\\n    Quote: Blackwell Ultra and Rubin: Target shipping dates for Blackwell Ultra chips are ramping up fast and the Vera Rubin architecture remains officially on track for late 2026, but the market is currently priced for absolute perfection.\"\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"control\": 3,\n  \"ruler_question\": {\n   \"question\": \"How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?\",\n   \"unit\": \"USD\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-12-31\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Below $550 billion\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is less than $550 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": 550000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"$550 billion to below $600 billion\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $550 billion and less than $600 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 550000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": 600000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"$600 billion to below $700 billion\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $600 billion and less than $700 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 600000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": 700000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"$700 billion to below $800 billion\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $700 billion and less than $800 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 700000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": 800000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"$800 billion or more\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $800 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 800000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": null\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"judged_question\": {\n   \"view_id\": \"v5\",\n   \"question\": \"How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?\",\n   \"looks_at\": \"Calendar 2026 data-center capital expenditures by the four largest AI hyperscalers\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-12-31\",\n   \"year\": \"2026\",\n   \"kind\": \"theme\"\n  },\n  \"judged_rungs\": [\n   {\n    \"position\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"Below $500 billion\",\n    \"condition\": \"Combined spending is below $500 billion.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": 500000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"$500 billion to below $600 billion\",\n    \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $500 billion and below $600 billion.\",\n    \"lo\": 500000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": 600000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 3,\n    \"label\": \"$600 billion to below $700 billion\",\n    \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $600 billion and below $700 billion.\",\n    \"lo\": 600000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": 700000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 4,\n    \"label\": \"$700 billion to below $800 billion\",\n    \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $700 billion and below $800 billion.\",\n    \"lo\": 700000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": 800000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 5,\n    \"label\": \"$800 billion or more\",\n    \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $800 billion.\",\n    \"lo\": 800000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i256\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Is Down 17% From Its High. Is the Artificial Intelligence (AI) Leader Finally Cheap?\",\n    \"passage\": \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"big four AI hyperscalers' own plans (2026); NVIDIA's claim (2027)\",\n     \"what\": \"data-center capital expenditure of the big four\",\n     \"how_much\": \"around $650bn (2026); more than $1 trillion (2027)\",\n     \"period\": \"calendar 2026; calendar 2027\",\n     \"status\": \"plan (2026); forecast by NVIDIA (2027)\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i232\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n    \"title\": \"A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout\",\n    \"passage\": \"2026 capex is guided up tp $190 billion\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"Alphabet / Google\",\n     \"what\": \"capital expenditure guidance\",\n     \"how_much\": \"up to $190bn\",\n     \"period\": \"2026\",\n     \"status\": \"forecast (guidance)\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i233\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n    \"title\": \"A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout\",\n    \"passage\": \"Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"Google Cloud\",\n     \"what\": \"revenue growth and backlog\",\n     \"how_much\": \"revenue +63%; backlog nearly doubled to over $460bn\",\n     \"period\": \"latest reported quarter\",\n     \"status\": \"happened\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i295\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-10\",\n    \"title\": \"Here are Friday's biggest analyst calls: Nvidia, SpaceX, Toll, Shopify, Twilio, Seagate, Chipotle & more\",\n    \"passage\": \"the company describing \\\"accelerating growth rates\\\" even as revenues approach the $100 bn per quarter mark\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA management, relayed by Morgan Stanley\",\n     \"what\": \"revenue growth rates accelerating\",\n     \"how_much\": \"quarterly revenue approaching $100 bn\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\"\n    }\n   }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\": [\n   \"- e38 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source The four largest AI hyperscalers ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] The four largest AI hyperscalers plan about $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\\n    Quote: The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year.\",\n   \"- e44 [third_party_view; Paul Triolo, quoted by CNBC; original source Paul Triolo ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] Paul Triolo said power supply is likely to remain the major constraint on U.S. AI data-center spending and suggested advanced systems may be ready when those power bottlenecks ease.\\n    Quote: With power supply likely to remain the major constraint on American AI data center spending, Triolo said, \\\"delays in getting to more advanced systems could just mean that the new systems are ready by the time the U.S. can work to overcome some of the critical bottlenecks on power now dogging the industry.\\\"\",\n   \"- e38 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source The four largest AI hyperscalers ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] The four largest AI hyperscalers plan about $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\\n    Quote: The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e64; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e51 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis forecasts annual AI capex above $2 trillion in 2028 and cumulative AI capex of about $11.1 trillion over 2024–2029, with credit markets as the main funding source.\\n    Quote: Annual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\",\n   \"- e64 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f 2026-07-06] NVIDIA management projected hyperscaler spending of $650 billion in calendar 2026.\\n    Quote: after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e38; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e72 [third_party_view; Allen Harris, the author; original source Goldman Sachs ; from gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512 2026-07-06] Goldman Sachs estimated annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in calendar 2026, grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, and total about $7.6 trillion cumulatively from 2026 through 2031.\\n    Quote: Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031.\",\n   \"- e38 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source The four largest AI hyperscalers ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] The four largest AI hyperscalers plan about $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\\n    Quote: The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e64, e99, e103; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e99 [third_party_view; fool.com analyst; original source fool.com analyst 2026-07-07; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] The author estimates that the four largest AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\\n    Quote: This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e38; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e101 [third_party_view; fool.com analyst; original source fool.com analyst 2026-07-07; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] The author expects Microsoft and TSMC to report strong earnings and provide strong forward guidance, which could lift Nvidia's stock.\\n    Quote: I think both of these companies will report strong earnings with great forward guidance, and that could cause Nvidia's stock to rise.\",\n   \"- e103 [third_party_view; fool.com (Keithen Drury); original source fool.com (Keithen Drury) 2026-07-07; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] The author estimated that the four largest AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and that spending in calendar 2027 could exceed $1 trillion.\\n    Quote: While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e38; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e106 [party_statement; 247Wallst.com; original source Google ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] Google guided 2026 capital expenditures to up to $190 billion.\\n    Quote: 2026 capex is guided up tp $190 billion.\",\n   \"- e176 [party_statement; 247WallSt. (David Moadel), quoting Jensen Huang; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:cbb9ff1a37ebb451f9321da5 2026-07-10] CEO Jensen Huang said the AI-factory buildout was accelerating at extraordinary speed.\\n    Quote: As CEO Jensen Huang framed it on the Q1 earnings call, “The buildout of AI factories — the largest infrastructure expansion in human history — is accelerating at extraordinary speed.”\",\n   \"- e284 [party_statement; 247wallst.com, Alex Sirois; original source Meta ; from gnews:221f52333c8f5e936e515c4d 2026-07-13] Meta raised its 2026 capital-expenditure guidance to $125 billion–$145 billion and launched Meta Compute, which rents excess internal AI capacity to external enterprises.\\n    Quote: Meta raised 2026 capex to $125 to $145 billion, then quietly launched Meta Compute, a service renting its excess internal AI capacity to external enterprises.\",\n   \"- e290 [third_party_view; Justin Pope, The Motley Fool; original source Meta Platforms, Microsoft, Alphabet, and Amazon ; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] Meta, Microsoft, Alphabet, and Amazon are planning higher capital expenditures in calendar 2026; no amount is given.\\n    Quote: Hyperscalers, including Meta Platforms , Microsoft , Alphabet , and Amazon , are planning higher capital expenditures in 2026.\",\n   \"- e355 [fact; EBC; original source Amazon ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] Amazon borrowed another $25 billion, according to EBC; the item linked the borrowing to a minor selloff in AI-related debt.\\n    Quote: Amazon recently made headlines by borrowing another $25 billion, causing a minor selloff in AI-related debt .\"\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"control\": 4,\n  \"ruler_question\": {\n   \"question\": \"How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?\",\n   \"unit\": \"USD\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2028-12-31\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Below $700 billion\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is less than $700 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": 700000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"$700 billion to below $800 billion\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $700 billion and less than $800 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 700000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": 800000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"$800 billion to below $900 billion\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $800 billion and less than $900 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 800000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": 900000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"$900 billion to below $1000 billion\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $900 billion and less than $1000 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 900000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": 1000000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"$1000 billion or more\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $1000 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 1000000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": null\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"judged_question\": {\n   \"view_id\": \"v6\",\n   \"question\": \"How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?\",\n   \"looks_at\": \"Calendar 2027 data-center capital expenditures by the four largest AI hyperscalers\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2028-12-31\",\n   \"year\": \"2027\",\n   \"kind\": \"theme\"\n  },\n  \"judged_rungs\": [\n   {\n    \"position\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"Below $700 billion\",\n    \"condition\": \"Combined spending is below $700 billion.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": 700000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"$700 billion to below $850 billion\",\n    \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $700 billion and below $850 billion.\",\n    \"lo\": 700000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": 850000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 3,\n    \"label\": \"$850 billion to below $1.15 trillion\",\n    \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $850 billion and below $1.15 trillion.\",\n    \"lo\": 850000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": 1150000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 4,\n    \"label\": \"$1.15 trillion to below $1.3 trillion\",\n    \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $1.15 trillion and below $1.3 trillion.\",\n    \"lo\": 1150000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": 1300000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 5,\n    \"label\": \"$1.3 trillion or more\",\n    \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $1.3 trillion.\",\n    \"lo\": 1300000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i256\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Is Down 17% From Its High. Is the Artificial Intelligence (AI) Leader Finally Cheap?\",\n    \"passage\": \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"big four AI hyperscalers' own plans (2026); NVIDIA's claim (2027)\",\n     \"what\": \"data-center capital expenditure of the big four\",\n     \"how_much\": \"around $650bn (2026); more than $1 trillion (2027)\",\n     \"period\": \"calendar 2026; calendar 2027\",\n     \"status\": \"plan (2026); forecast by NVIDIA (2027)\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i090\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-04\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Is Nearly Flat for 2026. Time to Cash Out, or Load Up on Shares?\",\n    \"passage\": \"Wall Street analysts project 82% revenue growth for this year and 41% next year\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"Wall Street analysts\",\n     \"what\": \"NVIDIA revenue growth forecast\",\n     \"how_much\": \"82% this year; 41% next year\",\n     \"period\": \"this year and next year (fiscal or calendar both accepted, D233)\",\n     \"status\": \"forecast\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i143\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\",\n    \"passage\": \"Some estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"unnamed estimates\",\n     \"what\": \"memory's share of data center spend\",\n     \"how_much\": \"about half\",\n     \"period\": \"2027\",\n     \"status\": \"forecast\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i161\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"CAPITAL IDEAS: The lag 7 — Is Micron the new Nvidia?\",\n    \"passage\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"Goldman Sachs estimate\",\n     \"what\": \"annual AI infrastructure capex (chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking)\",\n     \"how_much\": \"$765B (2026); $1.6T (2031); ~$7.6T cumulative 2026-2031\",\n     \"period\": \"2026; 2031; 2026-2031\",\n     \"condition\": \"could reach\",\n     \"status\": \"forecast\"\n    }\n   }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\": [\n   \"- e39 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] Nvidia projects that the four largest AI hyperscalers' data-center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027.\\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\",\n   \"- e39 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] Nvidia projects that the four largest AI hyperscalers' data-center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027.\\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e63; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e51 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis forecasts annual AI capex above $2 trillion in 2028 and cumulative AI capex of about $11.1 trillion over 2024–2029, with credit markets as the main funding source.\\n    Quote: Annual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\",\n   \"- e63 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f 2026-07-06] NVIDIA management forecast that hyperscaler spending will exceed $1 trillion in calendar 2027.\\n    Quote: During its last quarterly conference call, management forecast that it expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e39; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e63 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f 2026-07-06] NVIDIA management forecast that hyperscaler spending will exceed $1 trillion in calendar 2027.\\n    Quote: During its last quarterly conference call, management forecast that it expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e39, e100; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e100 [party_statement; fool.com analyst; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] Nvidia claims the four largest AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027.\\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e63; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e103 [third_party_view; fool.com (Keithen Drury); original source fool.com (Keithen Drury) 2026-07-07; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] The author estimated that the four largest AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and that spending in calendar 2027 could exceed $1 trillion.\\n    Quote: While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e38; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e176 [party_statement; 247WallSt. (David Moadel), quoting Jensen Huang; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:cbb9ff1a37ebb451f9321da5 2026-07-10] CEO Jensen Huang said the AI-factory buildout was accelerating at extraordinary speed.\\n    Quote: As CEO Jensen Huang framed it on the Q1 earnings call, “The buildout of AI factories — the largest infrastructure expansion in human history — is accelerating at extraordinary speed.”\",\n   \"- e39 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] Nvidia projects that the four largest AI hyperscalers' data-center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027.\\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e63, e207; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e207 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:07cf5835d106467db5448c13 2026-07-12] Nvidia expects AI hyperaccelerator spending to exceed $1 trillion in 2027.\\n    Quote: However, Nvidia told investors last quarter that it expects AI hyperaccelerator spending to rise to over $1 trillion next year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e39; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e100 [party_statement; fool.com analyst; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] Nvidia claims the four largest AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027.\\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e63, e288; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e288 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com author Rich Duprey; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Morgan Stanley forecasts that Google, Amazon, Microsoft and Meta will spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure in calendar 2027 and add 19.5 GW of compute capacity; Google is forecast to add 6.8 GW, versus roughly 6.7 GW added by the four in 2025.\\n    Quote: The analyst says Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and Meta are forecast to spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure next year, adding 19.5 gigawatts (GW) of incremental compute capacity. In 2025, they added roughly 6.7 GW. Google alone will add 6.8 GW, or more than the hyperscalers did combined two years ago.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e100; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\"\n  ]\n }\n]\n[\n {\n  \"control\": 5,\n  \"ruler_question\": {\n   \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA begin shipping its Rubin Ultra generation, in Kyber NVL144 racks, by December 31, 2027?\",\n   \"unit\": \"yes/no\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-12-31\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Yes\",\n     \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has begun shipping Rubin Ultra systems by December 31, 2027.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"No\",\n     \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has not begun shipping Rubin Ultra systems by December 31, 2027.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": null\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"judged_question\": {\n   \"view_id\": \"v8\",\n   \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA begin shipping its Kyber NVL144 rack architecture by December 31, 2028?\",\n   \"looks_at\": \"Whether NVIDIA begins shipping its Kyber NVL144 rack architecture by the end of 2028.\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2028-12-31\",\n   \"year\": \"2028\",\n   \"kind\": \"asset\"\n  },\n  \"judged_rungs\": [\n   {\n    \"position\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"Yes\",\n    \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has begun shipping the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture by 2028-12-31.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"No\",\n    \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has not begun shipping the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture by 2028-12-31.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i100\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n    \"passage\": \"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis (a research firm's report, not NVIDIA)\",\n     \"what\": \"Kyber NVL144 rack for Vera Rubin Ultra delayed; cause: PCB midplane manufacturability\",\n     \"how_much\": \"more than 12 months\",\n     \"period\": \"from 2027 to 2028\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i179\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\",\n    \"passage\": \"The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n     \"what\": \"four-die Rubin Ultra cancelled; two-die version remains\",\n     \"how_much\": \"roughly half the real-world compute\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\",\n     \"status_note\": \"cancelled\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i181\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\",\n    \"passage\": \"Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis, about NVIDIA's plan\",\n     \"what\": \"sell more Oberon-Rubin racks to cover the Kyber gap\",\n     \"status\": \"plan\",\n     \"status_note\": \"as claimed\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i101\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n    \"passage\": \"Our roadmap is intact\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"what\": \"rejects the SemiAnalysis report; says its roadmap (Rubin Ultra / Kyber in 2027) is unchanged\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i197\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated]\",\n    \"passage\": \"An Nvidia representative responded with a short statement to Tom's Hardware : \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA representative\",\n     \"what\": \"denies the report: roadmap intact\",\n     \"condition\": \"unclear whether the original or an already updated roadmap is meant\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\"\n    }\n   }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\": [\n   \"- e77 [rumour; Investing.com, summarizing SemiAnalysis’s weekend report; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack architecture would be delayed to 2028 because of PCB midplane manufacturing challenges.\\n    Quote: Supply chain research firm SemiAnalysis claimed in a weekend report that Nvidia's highly anticipated Kyber NVL144 rack architecture will be delayed to 2028 due to manufacturing challenges with its PCB midplane.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e82, e87; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e78 [party_statement; NVIDIA spokesperson, in a statement emailed to Investing.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] NVIDIA said its product roadmap remained intact, rejecting reports of delays to its next-generation server architectures.\\n    Quote: An Nvidia spokesperson swiftly refuted such claims in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, stating: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n   \"- e79 [rumour; Investing.com, summarizing SemiAnalysis’s weekend report; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis alleged that NVIDIA cancelled an alternative back-to-back rack design after operational pushback from hyperscale cloud clients.\\n    Quote: The research firm further alleged that Nvidia cancelled an alternative back-to-back rack design following heavy operational pushback from hyperscale cloud clients.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e84; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e81 [third_party_view; Investing.com, reporting analysts cited by Bloomberg; original source Analysts cited by Bloomberg ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] Analysts cited by Bloomberg said reported technical setbacks could severely limit NVIDIA’s future hardware scale-up capacity and widen the competitive opportunity for AMD’s MI500X and Broadcom-backed custom TPUs.\\n    Quote: According to analysts cited by Bloomberg, these rumored technical setbacks could severely cap the scale-up capacity of Nvidia's future hardware. This perceived vulnerability may create a wider competitive window for alternative AI platforms, specifically AMD's MI500X and Broadcom-backed custom TPUs.\",\n   \"- e82 [third_party_view; tikr.com (analyst); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia’s Kyber rack-scale server design has been delayed by more than a year, from 2027 to 2028.\\n    Quote: Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Kyber, Nvidia’s next rack-scale server design, has been pushed back more than a year , from 2027 to 2028.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e77; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e83 [third_party_view; tikr.com (analyst); original source tikr.com (analyst) 2026-07-06; from gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250 2026-07-06] The article reported that Nvidia’s NVL576 system may be delayed or ship only in small volumes.\\n    Quote: A larger version, called NVL576, which links eight racks together via optical connections, is also likely to be delayed or to ship only in small volumes.\",\n   \"- e84 [rumour; tikr.com (analyst); original source Cloud customers ; from gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250 2026-07-06] Cloud customers reportedly rejected Nvidia’s proposed design combining two current-generation racks, citing awkward design and high operating costs.\\n    Quote: Cloud customers reportedly rejected it because the design was awkward and the service was expensive to run.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e79; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e85 [third_party_view; tikr.com (analyst); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis said Nvidia currently lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra, potentially giving AMD and Google an opening in high-end AI chips.\\n    Quote: SemiAnalysis says this leaves Nvidia without a proven way to scale up Rubin Ultra for now, which could give rivals like AMD and Google an opening at the high end of the AI chip market.\",\n   \"- e87 [third_party_view; businesstimes.com.sg; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:c0432377817a1c00a1bbadf0 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia’s Kyber NVL144 had hit printed-circuit-board construction setbacks, contributing to a reported delay of more than a year.\\n    Quote: Research firm SemiAnalysis said in an X post that Nvidia’s Kyber NVL144 hit setbacks in the construction of printed circuit boards for the platform.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e77; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e88 [third_party_view; Shawn Oh, quoted by businesstimes.com.sg; original source Shawn Oh, head of Korea cash equities at NH Investment & Securities ; from gnews:c0432377817a1c00a1bbadf0 2026-07-06] Shawn Oh of NH Investment & Securities said the prospect of a Kyber NVL144 delay was raising uncertainty around Nvidia’s next-generation scale-out roadmap and creating a wider competitive window for alternative AI platforms.\\n    Quote: The prospect of a Kyber NVL144 delay together with other points in the post are “raising uncertainty around Nvidia’s next-generation scale-out roadmap and creating a wider competitive window for alternative AI platforms,” he added.\",\n   \"- e78 [party_statement; NVIDIA spokesperson, in a statement emailed to Investing.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] NVIDIA said its product roadmap remained intact, rejecting reports of delays to its next-generation server architectures.\\n    Quote: An Nvidia spokesperson swiftly refuted such claims in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, stating: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e98, e105; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e82 [third_party_view; tikr.com (analyst); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia’s Kyber rack-scale server design has been delayed by more than a year, from 2027 to 2028.\\n    Quote: Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Kyber, Nvidia’s next rack-scale server design, has been pushed back more than a year , from 2027 to 2028.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e77, e97, e104; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e97 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:b48593184704bbbe6eddbc03 2026-07-07] SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia was at least a year behind on manufacturing its next line of server racks.\\n    Quote: a research report claiming the AI leader was at least a year behind on manufacturing of its next line of server racks\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e82; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e98 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Nvidia 2026-07-06; from gnews:b48593184704bbbe6eddbc03 2026-07-07] Nvidia disputed SemiAnalysis’s report that its next-generation Kyber server rack was running into delays.\\n    Quote: the company disputed a report by SemiAnalysis that its next-gen Kyber server rack was running into delays\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e78; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e104 [third_party_view; 247Wallst.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 launch had moved from 2027 to 2028.\\n    Quote: SemiAnalysis reported on July 5–6, 2026 that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack-scale AI system has been delayed, from its planned 2027 launch to 2028.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e82; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e105 [party_statement; 247Wallst.com, quoting an NVIDIA spokesperson via Bloomberg; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] NVIDIA said its product roadmap remained intact, disputing the reported Kyber delay.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA publicly disputed the report on Monday, with a spokesperson telling Bloomberg its “roadmap is intact.”\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e78; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e87 [third_party_view; businesstimes.com.sg; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:c0432377817a1c00a1bbadf0 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia’s Kyber NVL144 had hit printed-circuit-board construction setbacks, contributing to a reported delay of more than a year.\\n    Quote: Research firm SemiAnalysis said in an X post that Nvidia’s Kyber NVL144 hit setbacks in the construction of printed circuit boards for the platform.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e77, e116; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e116 [third_party_view; Futuriom, relaying SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] SemiAnalysis said NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack system had hit manufacturing setbacks.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA’s stock also suffered this week because research firm SemiAnalysis stated in an X post that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack system has hit manufacturing setbacks—a situation that could be further impetus for NVIDIA to deploy its new credit and revenue-sharing model, creating a continuing flow of demand for its products.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e87; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e105 [party_statement; 247Wallst.com, quoting an NVIDIA spokesperson via Bloomberg; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] NVIDIA said its product roadmap remained intact, disputing the reported Kyber delay.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA publicly disputed the report on Monday, with a spokesperson telling Bloomberg its “roadmap is intact.”\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e78, e128; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e128 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:57de52b4f33b0c903d256f9e 2026-07-09] Nvidia denied reports that Kyber could be delayed until 2028, saying its roadmap remains on track; the article says Kyber is expected to launch in 2027.\\n    Quote: Recently, Nvidia faced reports that suggested its next-generation Kyber rack-scale AI infrastructure could be delayed until 2028. However, the company denied the speculation, maintaining that its roadmap remains on track. Designed for the Rubin Ultra platform, Kyber is expected to launch in 2027 as an integrated AI infrastructure system and a key component of Nvidia's long-term AI infrastructure s\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e105; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e105 [party_statement; 247Wallst.com, quoting an NVIDIA spokesperson via Bloomberg; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] NVIDIA said its product roadmap remained intact, disputing the reported Kyber delay.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA publicly disputed the report on Monday, with a spokesperson telling Bloomberg its “roadmap is intact.”\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e78, e128, e199; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e199 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1c3c0c58ed5ec3da43292f9b 2026-07-11] NVIDIA denied reports that its Kyber platform is being delayed; the item gives no date for the denial.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA Corp. (NASDAQ:NVDA) has denied reports that the Kyber platform is being delayed.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e105; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e200 [third_party_view; Jim Cramer, quoted by finance.yahoo.com; original source Semi Analysis ; from gnews:1c3c0c58ed5ec3da43292f9b 2026-07-11] Jim Cramer said Semi Analysis believes NVIDIA products could all be late because one part of its business is late; no specific product or timing was given.\\n    Quote: You know Semi Analysis does pretty good work, saying that everything could be late for NVIDIA because one particular part of their business is late.\",\n   \"- e77 [rumour; Investing.com, summarizing SemiAnalysis’s weekend report; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack architecture would be delayed to 2028 because of PCB midplane manufacturing challenges.\\n    Quote: Supply chain research firm SemiAnalysis claimed in a weekend report that Nvidia's highly anticipated Kyber NVL144 rack architecture will be delayed to 2028 due to manufacturing challenges with its PCB midplane.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e82, e87, e232; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e105 [party_statement; 247Wallst.com, quoting an NVIDIA spokesperson via Bloomberg; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] NVIDIA said its product roadmap remained intact, disputing the reported Kyber delay.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA publicly disputed the report on Monday, with a spokesperson telling Bloomberg its “roadmap is intact.”\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e78, e128, e199, e233; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e232 [third_party_view; fool.com (Geoffrey Seiler); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:3cd991655d4cdeaa4a578b84 2026-07-12] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack system could be delayed until 2028 because of manufacturing difficulties with a crucial circuit board.\\n    Quote: SemiAnalysis reported that its Kyber NVL144 next-gen AI rack system could be delayed until 2028 due to troubles with manufacturing a crucial circuit board.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e77; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e233 [party_statement; fool.com (Geoffrey Seiler); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3cd991655d4cdeaa4a578b84 2026-07-12] NVIDIA said its product roadmap remains intact despite questions about a possible Kyber NVL144 delay.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has come out and said its roadmap remains intact, although questions linger.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e105; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e234 [third_party_view; fool.com (Geoffrey Seiler); original source Geoffrey Seiler 2026-07-12; from gnews:3cd991655d4cdeaa4a578b84 2026-07-12] Geoffrey Seiler said a delay to NVIDIA’s Kyber rack system could give AMD and Alphabet an opportunity to gain share in the high-end AI market.\\n    Quote: A delay could help open the door for Advanced Micro Devices ( AMD +2.91% ) and Alphabet ( GOOGL -0.36% ) ( GOOG -0.55% ) to gain a foothold in the high end of the AI market.\",\n   \"- e235 [fact; fool.com (Geoffrey Seiler); original source AMD ; from gnews:3cd991655d4cdeaa4a578b84 2026-07-12] Geoffrey Seiler said AMD’s recent acquisition of memory-optimization software platform Mext, alongside its chiplet designs, positions AMD to offer a high-end server solution for inference.\\n    Quote: AMD's chiplet designs, which can package more memory onto its chips, and its recent acquisition of memory optimization software platform Mext, position it well to offer a high-end server solution designed specifically for inference.\",\n   \"- e236 [third_party_view; fool.com (Geoffrey Seiler); original source Geoffrey Seiler 2026-07-12; from gnews:3cd991655d4cdeaa4a578b84 2026-07-12] Geoffrey Seiler said Alphabet’s next-generation TPUs will be optimized for both AI training and inference.\\n    Quote: with its next generation, it will have chips optimized for both training and inference.\",\n   \"- e233 [party_statement; fool.com (Geoffrey Seiler); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3cd991655d4cdeaa4a578b84 2026-07-12] NVIDIA said its product roadmap remains intact despite questions about a possible Kyber NVL144 delay.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has come out and said its roadmap remains intact, although questions linger.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e105, e353; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e353 [party_statement; EBC, quoting an Nvidia spokesperson; original source Nvidia spokesperson ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] An Nvidia spokesperson said the company's roadmap was intact, responding to reports of possible delays to the Kyber NVL144 architecture.\\n    Quote: Although an Nvidia spokesperson quickly countered the reports, telling that the company's \\\"roadmap is intact,\\\" the seeds of doubt were already sown.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e233; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\"\n  ]\n }\n]\n[\n {\n  \"control\": 7,\n  \"ruler_question\": {\n   \"question\": \"What total amount of financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners will NVIDIA report outstanding at its FY2027 year-end?\",\n   \"unit\": \"USD\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-02-28\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Strongly bullish\",\n     \"condition\": \"The reported total is below $5 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": 5000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Bullish\",\n     \"condition\": \"The reported total is at least $5 billion and below $10 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 5000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": 10000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Neutral\",\n     \"condition\": \"The reported total is at least $10 billion and below $20 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 10000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": 20000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Bearish\",\n     \"condition\": \"The reported total is at least $20 billion and below $40 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 20000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": 40000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Strongly bearish\",\n     \"condition\": \"The reported total is at least $40 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 40000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": null\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"judged_question\": {\n   \"view_id\": \"v2\",\n   \"question\": \"At NVIDIA's FY2027 annual report, what amount will NVIDIA report for its outstanding customer data-center lease guarantees and related capacity-purchase commitments?\",\n   \"looks_at\": \"NVIDIA's credit-support and capacity-purchase commitments to customers, including the revenue-sharing and credit-support program\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-02-28\",\n   \"year\": \"FY2027\",\n   \"kind\": \"asset\"\n  },\n  \"judged_rungs\": [\n   {\n    \"position\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"Strongly bullish\",\n    \"condition\": \"The reported amount is below $3,500,000,000.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": 3500000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"Bullish\",\n    \"condition\": \"The reported amount is at least $3,500,000,000 and below $6,300,000,000.\",\n    \"lo\": 3500000000.0,\n    \"hi\": 6300000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 3,\n    \"label\": \"Neutral\",\n    \"condition\": \"The reported amount is at least $6,300,000,000 and below $10,000,000,000.\",\n    \"lo\": 6300000000.0,\n    \"hi\": 10000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 4,\n    \"label\": \"Bearish\",\n    \"condition\": \"The reported amount is at least $10,000,000,000 and below $20,000,000,000.\",\n    \"lo\": 10000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": 20000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 5,\n    \"label\": \"Strongly bearish\",\n    \"condition\": \"The reported amount is at least $20,000,000,000.\",\n    \"lo\": 20000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i120\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n    \"passage\": \"Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"Sharon AI disclosed\",\n     \"what\": \"total value of NVIDIA's backstop on Sharon's 72MW Australian AI factory (up to 40,000 GB300)\",\n     \"how_much\": \"$4.88B; implied floor ~$2.33/hr/GPU\",\n     \"period\": \"six years\",\n     \"condition\": \"up to 40,000 GB300s\",\n     \"status\": \"happened\",\n     \"status_note\": \"announced June 2026\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i122\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n    \"passage\": \"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"Firmus\",\n     \"what\": \"a second announced NVIDIA-backstopped cluster\",\n     \"how_much\": \"360MW\",\n     \"period\": \"announced June 29, 2026\",\n     \"status\": \"happened\",\n     \"status_note\": \"announced\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i123\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n    \"passage\": \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"Firmus\",\n     \"what\": \"expected customer revenue on the backstopped Batam cluster (part above the backstop is shared with NVIDIA)\",\n     \"how_much\": \"$25B-$30B\",\n     \"period\": \"over six years\",\n     \"status\": \"forecast\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i119\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n    \"passage\": \"Nvidia’s backstop program is only getting started, and we see announcements coming faster every quarter. Many Neoclouds are already deep in negotiations with Nvidia\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n     \"what\": \"more NVIDIA backstops exist but are not public; many neoclouds in negotiation; announcements accelerating\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\",\n     \"status_note\": \"existing undisclosed backstops; more deals are a forecast\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i005\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-01\",\n    \"title\": \"NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.\",\n    \"passage\": \"NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA (per The Information)\",\n     \"what\": \"negotiating to guarantee OpenAI's lease of a large Ohio data center\",\n     \"how_much\": \"the data center could cost as much as $500 billion if fully built (asset cost, not the guarantee amount)\",\n     \"condition\": \"in negotiation; nothing signed\",\n     \"status\": \"rumour\"\n    }\n   }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\": [\n   \"- e2 [fact; moomoo.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] NVIDIA announced a business model combining revenue sharing with credit support for AI cloud providers.\\n    Quote: On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and allowing them to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers, and corporate clients.\",\n   \"- e3 [rumour; moomoo.com; original source The Information 2026-07-01; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] Firmus and Sharon AI joined NVIDIA's revenue-sharing and credit-support program, according to the report, with three executives with existing NVIDIA business ties confirming the arrangement.\\n    Quote: GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement.\",\n   \"- e4 [party_statement; moomoo.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs\",\n   \"- e5 [party_statement; moomoo.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, planned to reach up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\",\n   \"- e7 [fact; moomoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] NVIDIA's filing submitted in May 2026, covering the quarter ended in April, reported that it added $3.5 billion in guarantees for customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\\n    Quote: According to a regulatory filing submitted by NVIDIA in May of this year (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\",\n   \"- e4 [party_statement; moomoo.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e12; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e5 [party_statement; moomoo.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, planned to reach up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e13; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e8 [fact; qz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] NVIDIA announced a revenue-sharing program intended to give AI startups, model builders, and enterprises access to its computing infrastructure without financing hardware buildouts.\\n    Quote: Nvidia $NVDA announced a revenue-sharing program on Wednesday designed to give AI startups, model builders, and enterprises access to its computing infrastructure without requiring them to finance hardware buildouts themselves.\",\n   \"- e9 [fact; qz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] Under NVIDIA's revenue-sharing arrangement, NVIDIA receives revenue from product sales and cloud operations provided by AI-cloud partners to end customers.\\n    Quote: Revenue from both product sales and cloud operations flows back to Nvidia under the arrangement, as AI cloud companies provide Nvidia -powered services to their end customers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e16, e19; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e10 [party_statement; qz.com; original source Colette Kress ; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress described the program's income as a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream and said it lets customers access large-scale compute without handling site selection, power procurement, construction, and hardware bring-up.\\n    Quote: Kress, writing in a blog post, called the resulting income a \\\"recurring, usage-linked earnings stream\\\" for Nvidia ; on the customer side, she said the model removes the need to navigate site selection, power procurement, construction, and hardware bring-up before accessing large-scale compute.\",\n   \"- e11 [fact; qz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] NVIDIA's offering includes token credits that let startups access compute resources without upfront capital outlays.\\n    Quote: Token credits are also part of the offering, giving startups a way to access compute resources without upfront capital outlays.\",\n   \"- e12 [fact; qz.com; original source NVIDIA; Sharon AI ; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] NVIDIA named Sharon AI as an initial partner, and Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: Nvidia named two initial partners. Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4, e21, e27; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e13 [fact; qz.com; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] Firmus Technologies plans a campus in Batam, Indonesia, with capacity for up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs and 360 megawatts of power.\\n    Quote: A campus being developed by Firmus Technologies in Batam, Indonesia is planned at a capacity of up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs and 360 megawatts of power.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5, e17, e28; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e14 [party_statement; qz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] NVIDIA said AI demand is shifting from model development toward production inference workloads that require continuously operating infrastructure at scale.\\n    Quote: Nvidia framed the program as a response to a structural shift in AI demand, from model development toward production inference workloads that require continuously operating infrastructure at scale.\",\n   \"- e15 [fact; CNBC; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] NVIDIA said it was introducing a partnership program to connect AI startups with computing infrastructure from AI cloud service providers.\\n    Quote: Chipmaker Nvidia is expanding its support for fast-growing AI startups by introducing a partnership program that leverages its network of AI cloud service providers to offer these firms access to critical computing infrastructure, the company said Thursday.\",\n   \"- e16 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] Under the announced program, cloud-based AI firms, model builders and other enterprises will share product and cloud revenue with NVIDIA.\\n    Quote: Cloud-based AI firms, model builders and other enterprises will share both product and cloud revenue with Nvidia, which is positioning itself as an intermediary helping startups gain direct access to full-stack computing powered by Nvidia chips.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e9, e22; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e17 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Sharon AI and Firmus Technologies 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA GPUs; Firmus Technologies says it is building a data center in Batam expected to reach 360 MW and house up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia GPUs, while AI infrastructure company Firmus Technologies says it is building a data center in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e13, e20, e24; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e22 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] NVIDIA announced a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to help startups access compute.\\n    Quote: In a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress, the company pitched the “revenue-sharing and credit-support model” as a way to open compute access to startups that can’t finance it themselves.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e16; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e23 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source Sharon AI 2026-06-12; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Sharon AI disclosed a six-year agreement covering 72 MW of new Australian data-center capacity, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: In an 8-K filing dated June 12th, Sharon AI disclosed that the agreement runs for six years and covers 72 MW of new Australian data center capacity built to Nvidia's DSX AI factory design, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e24 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Firmus is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, Firmus is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, that’s expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e17; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e26 [fact; mitrade.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-02; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] NVIDIA announced an AI compute framework using revenue-sharing and credit-support agreements to help cloud providers deploy advanced hardware.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA’s new AI compute framework allows cloud providers to deploy advanced hardware using revenue-sharing and credit-support agreements.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e19; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e27 [fact; mitrade.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e12; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e28 [fact; mitrade.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] Firmus is developing an Indonesia campus that could support approximately 170,000 GPUs and 360 megawatts of power capacity.\\n    Quote: Firmus is developing a major campus in Indonesia that could support approximately 170,000 GPUs and 360 megawatts of power capacity.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e13; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e29 [third_party_view; SixSigmaCapital; original source SixSigmaCapital 2026-07-02; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] SixSigmaCapital said NVIDIA’s framework gives neoclouds greater certainty but less upside, and reduces NVIDIA’s reliance on hyperscalers.\\n    Quote: $NVDA Simplistically what this means for neoclouds,in my view, is it gives them certainty but takes away some of the upside. I think that’s a good deal for them.Also reduces $NVDA reliance on hyper scalers.\",\n   \"- e23 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source Sharon AI 2026-06-12; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Sharon AI disclosed a six-year agreement covering 72 MW of new Australian data-center capacity, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: In an 8-K filing dated June 12th, Sharon AI disclosed that the agreement runs for six years and covers 72 MW of new Australian data center capacity built to Nvidia's DSX AI factory design, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21, e34; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e24 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Firmus is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, Firmus is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, that’s expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e17, e36; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e27 [fact; mitrade.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e12, e33; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e30 [party_statement; mlq.ai; original source Nvidia 2026-07-01; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Nvidia announced the AI Compute Partnership on July 1, 2026, to backstop neocloud customers' GPU deployments in return for a recurring share of cloud revenue generated by those GPUs.\\n    Quote: Nvidia on July 1 unveiled a new financing vehicle it calls the \\\"AI Compute Partnership,\\\" under which the chipmaker acts as a financial backstop for neocloud customers' GPU deployments and, in return, takes a recurring share of the cloud revenue those GPUs generate [1] .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e31 [party_statement; mlq.ai; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Under Nvidia's AI Compute Partnership, Nvidia agrees to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate, backstopping lenders' exposure to neocloud deployments.\\n    Quote: The backstop mechanism works by Nvidia agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate, effectively de-risking lender exposure and making it easier for smaller cloud operators to secure capital for large GPU deployments [1] [3] .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e18; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e32 [party_statement; mlq.ai; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Nvidia did not disclose the revenue-share percentage for the AI Compute Partnership and declined to provide details beyond its blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress.\\n    Quote: The revenue-share percentage was not disclosed; Nvidia declined to provide details beyond its blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress [3]\",\n   \"- e33 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Sharon AI and Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] The AI Compute Partnership's first adopters include Sharon AI, which plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW Batam campus targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI (NASDAQ: SHAZ), an Australian sovereign-cloud provider planning up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [2] .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e27; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e34 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under a six-year agreement with Nvidia.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI, which listed on the Nasdaq in February 2026 via a $125 million IPO, is deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under a six-year agreement with Nvidia [2] [6] .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e23; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e35 [fact; mlq.ai; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Sharon AI raised $1.6 billion in a June 2026 private placement to fund its Nvidia-based AI factory.\\n    Quote: The company raised an additional $1.6 billion in private placement in June 2026 to fund its Nvidia-based AI factory\",\n   \"- e36 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Firmus Technologies is constructing a Batam campus to Nvidia DGX SuperPOD (DSX) specifications, with plans for up to 360MW of capacity and up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Quote: Firmus Technologies, a private company, is constructing a campus in Batam, Indonesia, built to Nvidia's DGX SuperPOD (DSX) reference architecture specifications. The facility is expected to scale to 360MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [3] .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e24; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e33 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Sharon AI and Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] The AI Compute Partnership's first adopters include Sharon AI, which plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW Batam campus targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI (NASDAQ: SHAZ), an Australian sovereign-cloud provider planning up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [2] .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e27, e42; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e36 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Firmus Technologies is constructing a Batam campus to Nvidia DGX SuperPOD (DSX) specifications, with plans for up to 360MW of capacity and up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Quote: Firmus Technologies, a private company, is constructing a campus in Batam, Indonesia, built to Nvidia's DGX SuperPOD (DSX) reference architecture specifications. The facility is expected to scale to 360MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [3] .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e24, e43; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e40 [fact; datacenters.economictimes.indiatimes.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4 2026-07-04] Nvidia launched a revenue-sharing financing model for AI cloud providers; the report gives no amount or date for the launch.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has launched a revenue-sharing financing model aimed at AI cloud providers, facilitating access to its computing infrastructure.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e19; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e41 [fact; datacenters.economictimes.indiatimes.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4 2026-07-04] Nvidia's financing model lets AI cloud operators procure its infrastructure with credit support, with revenue sharing linked to cloud services sold using that capacity.\\n    Quote: Under this financing structure, AI cloud operators can procure Nvidia infrastructure with credit support, with revenue sharing linked to the cloud services sold based on that capacity.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e19; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e42 [fact; datacenters.economictimes.indiatimes.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4 2026-07-04] Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs; the report gives no deployment date.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs,\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e33; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e43 [fact; datacenters.economictimes.indiatimes.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4 2026-07-04] Firmus is developing a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to reach 360 MW and accommodate up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs; no completion date is given.\\n    Quote: Firmus is developing a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e36; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e8 [fact; qz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] NVIDIA announced a revenue-sharing program intended to give AI startups, model builders, and enterprises access to its computing infrastructure without financing hardware buildouts.\\n    Quote: Nvidia $NVDA announced a revenue-sharing program on Wednesday designed to give AI startups, model builders, and enterprises access to its computing infrastructure without requiring them to finance hardware buildouts themselves.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e50; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e17 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Sharon AI and Firmus Technologies 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA GPUs; Firmus Technologies says it is building a data center in Batam expected to reach 360 MW and house up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia GPUs, while AI infrastructure company Firmus Technologies says it is building a data center in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e13, e20, e24, e60; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e33 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Sharon AI and Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] The AI Compute Partnership's first adopters include Sharon AI, which plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW Batam campus targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI (NASDAQ: SHAZ), an Australian sovereign-cloud provider planning up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [2] .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e27, e42, e68; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e34 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under a six-year agreement with Nvidia.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI, which listed on the Nasdaq in February 2026 via a $125 million IPO, is deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under a six-year agreement with Nvidia [2] [6] .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e23, e59; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e40 [fact; datacenters.economictimes.indiatimes.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4 2026-07-04] Nvidia launched a revenue-sharing financing model for AI cloud providers; the report gives no amount or date for the launch.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has launched a revenue-sharing financing model aimed at AI cloud providers, facilitating access to its computing infrastructure.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e19, e52; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e41 [fact; datacenters.economictimes.indiatimes.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4 2026-07-04] Nvidia's financing model lets AI cloud operators procure its infrastructure with credit support, with revenue sharing linked to cloud services sold using that capacity.\\n    Quote: Under this financing structure, AI cloud operators can procure Nvidia infrastructure with credit support, with revenue sharing linked to the cloud services sold based on that capacity.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e19, e69; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e50 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:68a3ed21f77b935d9fa7ec5e 2026-07-06] NVIDIA had launched a revenue-sharing compute program for AI startups; the item gives no launch date or program amount.\\n    Quote: The chipmaker, fresh off launching a revenue-sharing compute program for AI startups, has faced this script before.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e8; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e52 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has started providing neoclouds take-or-pay minimum-revenue guarantees on GPU rental capacity in exchange for a share of revenue above the backstop level.\\n    Quote: This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e40; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e53 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has started backstopping neocloud data-center leases.\\n    Quote: Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\",\n   \"- e54 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis says NVIDIA’s backstop program typically lasts six years and commits NVIDIA to purchase compute at pre-agreed prices that vary over the term.\\n    Quote: Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period.\",\n   \"- e55 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis says lenders underwrite NVIDIA-backstopped GPU financing to a minimum 1.3x debt-service coverage ratio, typically corresponding to a 70–80% loan-to-value ratio.\\n    Quote: When it comes to the Nvidia backstop, banks look at the DSCR ratios assuming revenue earned under the scenario where the backstop is activated. They are looking for a DSCR of at least 1.3x, particularly for the first few years of GPU financing agreement and size their loans to ensure that this bar is met. This typically corresponds to a 70-80% loan to value (LTV) ratio.\",\n   \"- e56 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis models a 25.4% project IRR for a neocloud’s one-year-or-shorter GPU rental book with NVIDIA’s backstop, potentially higher if rental rates decline less than modeled.\\n    Quote: We see that the short-term 1y rental book scenario clearly offers the best IRR among the backstopped scenarios at 25.4%, and these IRRs could realize even higher if the rental market turns out to be strong enough such that 1y and shorter rental rates decay by less than we modeled in the curve below.\",\n   \"- e57 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis models up to a 40.7% IRR for one-year GPU rentals without a backstop, versus zero or negative IRRs if a neocloud must rent to NVIDIA under the backstop.\\n    Quote: As expected, the IRRs are higher without a backstop – reaching as high as 40.7% when renting for 1y tenors with no backstop. But if Neoclouds activate the backstop and rent to Nvidia at those prices, they can expect to earn zero or negative IRRs.\",\n   \"- e58 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis models neocloud profit-before-tax margins falling from 14.8% to 5.4% when financing cost rises from 5.62% to 10%.\\n    Quote: In our modelling, we show that increasing funding from an all-in cost of 5.62% to an all-in cost of 10% drops PBT margins from 14.8% down to only 5.4%.\",\n   \"- e59 [party_statement; SemiAnalysis; original source SharonAI ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SharonAI’s June 2026 Australia AI-factory deal includes a six-year NVIDIA backstop for up to 40,000 GB300s valued at $4.88 billion, or about $2.33 per GPU-hour on average; SharonAI says its footprint is set to reach 132 MW, with 102 MW contracted, and guides to more than 55,000 NVIDIA GPUs by mid-2027.\\n    Quote: The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period. Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e34; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e60 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source Firmus 2026-06-29; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] Firmus announced a 360 MW AI cluster project in Batam, Indonesia, on June 29, 2026, as part of the NVIDIA-backstop rollout.\\n    Quote: Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e17; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e61 [party_statement; SemiAnalysis; original source Firmus ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] Firmus expects $25–30 billion of customer revenue over six years from its project; SemiAnalysis expects Firmus to share some revenue above the NVIDIA backstop level with NVIDIA.\\n    Quote: Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\",\n   \"- e67 [fact; futurumgroup.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] NVIDIA introduced a revenue-sharing and credit-support model for AI-cloud operators deploying AI factories on July 1, 2026.\\n    Quote: On July 1, 2026, NVIDIA introduced a business model that lets AI cloud operators deploy large-scale, multi-tenant AI factories through a combination of revenue sharing and credit support, with the resulting facilities built as DSX AI factories.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e68 [third_party_view; futurumgroup.com (analyst); original source futurumgroup.com (analyst) ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] Sharon AI is deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs in new Australian capacity, and Firmus is building a 360-megawatt DSX-aligned campus in Batam targeting up to 170,000 GPUs; both are among the first partners in NVIDIA’s model.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI, deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs across new capacity in Australia, and Firmus, building a 360-megawatt DSX-aligned AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 GPUs, are among the first partners.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e33; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e69 [third_party_view; futurumgroup.com (analyst); original source futurumgroup.com (analyst) ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] Under the model introduced July 1, NVIDIA earns its standard hardware margin plus a recurring share of cloud income generated by supported capacity.\\n    Quote: Under the July 1 revenue-sharing and credit-support model, NVIDIA earns its standard hardware margin plus a recurring, usage-linked share of the cloud income the supported capacity generates.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e41; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e70 [party_statement; futurumgroup.com (analyst); original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said the cost of a 1-gigawatt AI factory has risen from $20–30 billion to $50–60 billion and will soon reach $80–100 billion per gigawatt; he also expects 100 gigawatts to be online before the decade ends.\\n    Quote: Huang has said a 1-gigawatt AI factory that once cost $20–30 billion now runs $50–60 billion and will soon reach $80–100 billion per gigawatt, with 100 gigawatts expected online before the end of the decade.\",\n   \"- e71 [third_party_view; futurumgroup.com (analyst); original source futurumgroup.com (analyst) ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] Firmus has $25–30 billion in committed off-take, according to the analyst, which the analyst says may or may not convert into recognized revenue on schedule.\\n    Quote: Whether Firmus’s $25–30 billion in committed off-take converts into recognized revenue on schedule.\",\n   \"- e33 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Sharon AI and Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] The AI Compute Partnership's first adopters include Sharon AI, which plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW Batam campus targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI (NASDAQ: SHAZ), an Australian sovereign-cloud provider planning up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [2] .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e27, e42, e68, e114; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e36 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Firmus Technologies is constructing a Batam campus to Nvidia DGX SuperPOD (DSX) specifications, with plans for up to 360MW of capacity and up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Quote: Firmus Technologies, a private company, is constructing a campus in Batam, Indonesia, built to Nvidia's DGX SuperPOD (DSX) reference architecture specifications. The facility is expected to scale to 360MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [3] .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e24, e43, e115; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e113 [party_statement; Futuriom, quoting NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress and VP of Global AI Clouds and Infrastructure Ecosystem Raj Mirpuri; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress and VP of Global AI Clouds and Infrastructure Ecosystem Raj Mirpuri 2026-07-08; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress and VP of Global AI Clouds and Infrastructure Ecosystem Raj Mirpuri said the new AI-cloud model provides NVIDIA with standard product revenue and a share of cloud revenue on supported capacity.\\n    Quote: “This new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services, with NVIDIA earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity. This structure accelerates \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e19; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e114 [fact; Futuriom; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] Sharon AI plans to buy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs through NVIDIA's AI-cloud revenue-sharing and credit-support plan.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI, based in Australia, will buy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under the plan.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e33; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e115 [fact; Futuriom; original source Firmus ; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] Firmus is building a DSX AI factory in Indonesia expected to scale to 360 megawatts and contain up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Firmus, based in Indoesia, is building a DSX AI factory there that’s expected to scale to 360 megawatts and contain up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e36; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e118 [third_party_view; Futuriom; original source Futuriom 2026-07-08; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] Futuriom judged that NVIDIA's new AI-cloud business model could increase GPU demand from startups and other small enterprises.\\n    Quote: Futuriom Take: NVIDIA’s new business model for AI cloud providers could boost demand for its GPUs among startups and other small enterprises.\",\n   \"- e13 [fact; qz.com; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] Firmus Technologies plans a campus in Batam, Indonesia, with capacity for up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs and 360 megawatts of power.\\n    Quote: A campus being developed by Firmus Technologies in Batam, Indonesia is planned at a capacity of up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs and 360 megawatts of power.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5, e17, e28, e359; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e61 [party_statement; SemiAnalysis; original source Firmus ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] Firmus expects $25–30 billion of customer revenue over six years from its project; SemiAnalysis expects Firmus to share some revenue above the NVIDIA backstop level with NVIDIA.\\n    Quote: Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e360; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e358 [party_statement; Jakarta Globe, quoting Airlangga Hartarto; original source Airlangga Hartarto, Indonesia Chief Economic Affairs Minister ; from gnews:e001119c6342373f6b1f3cf4 2026-07-14] Indonesia Chief Economic Affairs Minister Airlangga Hartarto said the country has about 1.3 GW of planned data-center capacity, including the Batam project, representing $15 billion to $20 billion of investment; current operational capacity is about 580 MW.\\n    Quote: Indonesia currently has around 580 MW of operational data center capacity. The additional 1.3 GW that is in the pipeline, including the Batam project, represents investments of around $15 billion (Rp 271 trillion) to $20 billion\",\n   \"- e359 [fact; Jakarta Globe; original source Firmus Technologies and DayOne ; from gnews:e001119c6342373f6b1f3cf4 2026-07-14] Firmus Technologies and DayOne are developing the Nvidia DSX AI Factory Campus, a planned 360 MW AI data-center complex in Batam.\\n    Quote: The Australian AI infrastructure company is partnering with Singapore-based data center operator DayOne to develop the Nvidia DSX AI Factory Campus, a 360-megawatt AI data center complex that is expected to become one of Southeast Asia's largest AI computing hubs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e13; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e360 [third_party_view; Jakarta Globe, relaying The Straits Times; original source The Straits Times ; from gnews:e001119c6342373f6b1f3cf4 2026-07-14] The Straits Times projected that the Batam campus could generate up to $30 billion in off-take agreements during its first six years of operation.\\n    Quote: According to The Straits Times , the project is projected to generate as much as $30 billion in off-take agreements during its first six years of operation.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e61; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e361 [party_statement; Jakarta Globe; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:e001119c6342373f6b1f3cf4 2026-07-14] Firmus plans to deploy about 170,000 of Nvidia's latest-generation GPUs in phases from Q1 2027 through early 2028 for AI computing capacity in Indonesia.\\n    Quote: Under the partnership, Firmus plans to deploy about 170,000 of Nvidia's latest-generation graphics processing units (GPUs) in phases from the first quarter of 2027 through early 2028, providing AI computing capacity for startups and fast-growing AI companies.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e33; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\"\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"control\": 8,\n  \"ruler_question\": {\n   \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a guarantee of OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease by 2027-06-30?\",\n   \"unit\": \"yes/no\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-06-30\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Yes\",\n     \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has signed the guarantee by the due day.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"No\",\n     \"condition\": \"It has not.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"judged_question\": {\n   \"view_id\": \"v2\",\n   \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee for OpenAI’s Ohio data-center lease by July 1, 2027?\",\n   \"looks_at\": \"Whether NVIDIA signs a financial guarantee for OpenAI’s Ohio data-center lease\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-07-01\",\n   \"year\": \"2027\",\n   \"kind\": \"asset\"\n  },\n  \"judged_rungs\": [\n   {\n    \"position\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"No\",\n    \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has not signed a financial guarantee for the Ohio data-center lease by 2027-07-01.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"Yes\",\n    \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has signed a financial guarantee for the Ohio data-center lease by 2027-07-01.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i005\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-01\",\n    \"title\": \"NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.\",\n    \"passage\": \"NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA (per The Information)\",\n     \"what\": \"negotiating to guarantee OpenAI's lease of a large Ohio data center\",\n     \"how_much\": \"the data center could cost as much as $500 billion if fully built (asset cost, not the guarantee amount)\",\n     \"condition\": \"in negotiation; nothing signed\",\n     \"status\": \"rumour\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i006\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-01\",\n    \"title\": \"NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.\",\n    \"passage\": \"NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"what\": \"guarantees of customer data center leases, in exchange for rights to buy their shares\",\n     \"how_much\": \"an additional $3.5 billion\",\n     \"period\": \"quarter ended April 2026 (filed May)\",\n     \"status\": \"happened\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i116\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n    \"passage\": \"Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n     \"what\": \"NVIDIA backstops datacenter leases for neoclouds (a second kind of obligation)\",\n     \"status\": \"happened\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i119\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n    \"passage\": \"Nvidia’s backstop program is only getting started, and we see announcements coming faster every quarter. Many Neoclouds are already deep in negotiations with Nvidia\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n     \"what\": \"more NVIDIA backstops exist but are not public; many neoclouds in negotiation; announcements accelerating\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\",\n     \"status_note\": \"existing undisclosed backstops; more deals are a forecast\"\n    }\n   }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\": [\n   \"- e6 [rumour; moomoo.com; original source The Information ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] The Information recently reported that NVIDIA was negotiating to guarantee OpenAI’s lease of a large Ohio data center; the facility could cost up to $500 billion if fully built out at current prices.\\n    Quote: According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power, and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\",\n   \"- e13 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] Nvidia said on 2026-07-02 that it was introducing a partnership program using AI cloud providers to give AI startups access to computing infrastructure.\\n    Quote: Chipmaker Nvidia is expanding its support for fast-growing AI startups by introducing a partnership program that leverages its network of AI cloud service providers to offer these firms access to critical computing infrastructure, the company said Thursday.\",\n   \"- e14 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] Under Nvidia’s new AI-cloud partnership program, participating firms will share product and cloud revenue with Nvidia; the article does not specify an amount or period.\\n    Quote: Cloud-based AI firms, model builders and other enterprises will share both product and cloud revenue with Nvidia, which is positioning itself as an intermediary helping startups gain direct access to full-stack computing powered by Nvidia chips.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1, e19, e25; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e15 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Sharon AI and Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] The two initial AI-cloud partners plan to provide compute for Nvidia’s program: Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia GPUs, and Firmus is building a Batam data center expected to reach 360 megawatts and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia GPUs, while AI infrastructure company Firmus Technologies says it is building a data center in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e20 [fact; Tom's Hardware author Luke James; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] NVIDIA announced a revenue-sharing and credit-support model in which participating AI clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure and NVIDIA receives a percentage of revenue generated by that infrastructure, in addition to hardware sales.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has announced a new business model under which it’ll be able to double-dip for revenue on the same silicon: once when its partner AI clouds use its hardware, and again as an ongoing percentage of the revenue that hardware generates. In a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress, the company pitched the “revenue-sharing and credit-support model” as a way to open compute access to startups \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e19; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e21 [fact; Tom's Hardware author Luke James; original source Sharon AI 2026-06-12; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Sharon AI disclosed an agreement with NVIDIA running for six years and covering 72 MW of new Australian data-center capacity, with capacity to scale to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: In an 8-K filing dated June 12th, Sharon AI disclosed that the agreement runs for six years and covers 72 MW of new Australian data center capacity built to Nvidia's DSX AI factory design, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e9; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e22 [fact; Tom's Hardware author Luke James; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Firmus is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, Firmus is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, that’s expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e25 [party_statement; NVIDIA, in a post quoted by mitrade.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-02; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] NVIDIA said it is partnering with AI clouds on large-scale, multi-tenant AI factories using revenue-sharing and credit-support arrangements; the statement did not give an amount or period.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA is partnering with AI clouds to deploy large‑scale, multi‑tenant AI factories through revenue-sharing and credit-support. This opens up compute access to the… pic.twitter.com/PSQdTQJTga\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e14; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e26 [fact; mitrade.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs; no deployment date was given.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e27 [fact; mitrade.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] Firmus is developing an Indonesia campus that could support approximately 170,000 GPUs and 360 MW of power capacity; no completion date was given.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, Firmus is developing a major campus in Indonesia that could support approximately 170,000 GPUs and 360 megawatts of power capacity.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e28 [third_party_view; SixSigmaCapital, in a post quoted by mitrade.com; original source SixSigmaCapital 2026-07-02; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] SixSigmaCapital said NVIDIA’s model gives neoclouds more certainty but less upside, and reduces NVIDIA’s reliance on hyperscalers; it gave no figures.\\n    Quote: $NVDA Simplistically what this means for neoclouds,in my view, is it gives them certainty but takes away some of the upside. I think that’s a good deal for them.Also reduces $NVDA reliance on hyper scalers. So $META news yesterday almost irrelevant now too https://t.co/Xpz1WfCHhx\",\n   \"- e20 [fact; Tom's Hardware author Luke James; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] NVIDIA announced a revenue-sharing and credit-support model in which participating AI clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure and NVIDIA receives a percentage of revenue generated by that infrastructure, in addition to hardware sales.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has announced a new business model under which it’ll be able to double-dip for revenue on the same silicon: once when its partner AI clouds use its hardware, and again as an ongoing percentage of the revenue that hardware generates. In a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress, the company pitched the “revenue-sharing and credit-support model” as a way to open compute access to startups \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e19, e52; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e21 [fact; Tom's Hardware author Luke James; original source Sharon AI 2026-06-12; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Sharon AI disclosed an agreement with NVIDIA running for six years and covering 72 MW of new Australian data-center capacity, with capacity to scale to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: In an 8-K filing dated June 12th, Sharon AI disclosed that the agreement runs for six years and covers 72 MW of new Australian data center capacity built to Nvidia's DSX AI factory design, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e9, e44; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e22 [fact; Tom's Hardware author Luke James; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Firmus is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, Firmus is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, that’s expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4, e54; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e78 [party_statement; SemiAnalysis; original source SharonAI ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SharonAI said its footprint is set to reach 132MW, including 102MW contracted, and it guided to more than 55,000 NVIDIA GPUs in total by mid-2027, including 40,000 backstopped GB300s.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e15 (); both are in force: The two initial AI-cloud partners plan to provide compute for Nvidia’s program: Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia GPUs, and Firmus is building a Batam data center expected to reach 360 megawatts and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\",\n   \"- e230 [party_statement; Futuriom, quoting NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress and VP of Global AI Clouds and Infrastructure Ecosystem Raj Mirpuri; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress and VP of Global AI Clouds and Infrastructure Ecosystem Raj Mirpuri ; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress and VP Raj Mirpuri said the new AI-cloud partnership model combines credit support with revenue sharing, with NVIDIA earning standard product revenue plus a share of cloud revenue on supported capacity.\\n    Quote: “This new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services, with NVIDIA earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity. This structure accelerates \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e19; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e231 [party_statement; Futuriom; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] NVIDIA said Sharon AI signed onto its new AI-cloud plan and will buy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA states that two AI cloud providers have already signed on for the new deal: Sharon AI, based in Australia, will buy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under the plan.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e44; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e232 [fact; Futuriom; original source Firmus ; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] Firmus is building a DSX AI factory in Indonesia expected to scale to 360 MW and contain up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Firmus, based in Indoesia, is building a DSX AI factory there that’s expected to scale to 360 megawatts and contain up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e54; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\"\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"control\": 9,\n  \"ruler_question\": {\n   \"question\": \"Will Nvidia begin shipping its Rubin generation by December 31, 2026?\",\n   \"unit\": \"yes/no\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2026-12-31\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Yes\",\n     \"condition\": \"Nvidia has begun shipping Rubin by December 31, 2026.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"No\",\n     \"condition\": \"Nvidia has not begun shipping Rubin by December 31, 2026.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": null\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"judged_question\": {\n   \"view_id\": \"v3\",\n   \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA begin shipping its Vera Rubin platform by December 31, 2026?\",\n   \"looks_at\": \"Whether the Vera Rubin platform begins shipping by the end of 2026\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2026-12-31\",\n   \"year\": \"2026\",\n   \"kind\": \"asset\"\n  },\n  \"judged_rungs\": [\n   {\n    \"position\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"Yes\",\n    \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has begun shipping Vera Rubin by December 31, 2026.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"No\",\n    \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has not begun shipping Vera Rubin by December 31, 2026.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i105\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n    \"passage\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"what\": \"Vera Rubin systems in full production; shipping to eight cloud partners including AWS, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud\",\n     \"period\": \"this fall (2026)\",\n     \"status\": \"happened (full production); plan (shipping this fall)\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i219\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia Vera Rubin rack costs $7.8M — memory is why\",\n    \"passage\": \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"what\": \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 first shipments and volume ramp\",\n     \"period\": \"first shipments Q3 2026; volume ramp Q4 2026\",\n     \"status\": \"plan\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i202\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated]\",\n    \"passage\": \"Nvidia's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, aren't part of the reported delay.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"Tom's Hardware\",\n     \"what\": \"scope of the reported delay: Rubin Ultra/Kyber only; 2026 Rubin on Oberon unaffected\",\n     \"period\": \"2026\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i207\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia Delays Next-Generation Kyber Server Architecture as Manufacturing Hurdles Give Rivals a Rare Opening\",\n    \"passage\": \"the company’s current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and set to start shipping this fall to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA (as restated)\",\n     \"what\": \"Rubin systems in full production; first shipments to eight cloud partners incl. AWS, Azure, Google Cloud\",\n     \"period\": \"this fall\",\n     \"status\": \"happened\",\n     \"status_note\": \"in full production; shipping this fall is a plan\"\n    }\n   }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\": [\n   \"- e23 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-02; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] The author says NVIDIA’s Vera Rubin platform is in full production and could begin shipping by the end of 2026.\\n    Quote: Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year.\",\n   \"- e45 [party_statement; Jonathan Sayers; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a8fab49de53d125314a6688a 2026-07-04] NVIDIA announced that its DSX AI factories are hosting Rubin AI infrastructure in a design with no fans or cold aisles.\\n    Quote: it was announced that Nvidia DSX AI factories are hosting Nvidia's Rubin AI infrastructure with no fans or cold aisles.\",\n   \"- e61 [rumour; CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] NVIDIA’s current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are due to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including AWS, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\\n    Quote: Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\",\n   \"- e64 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-05; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] The author says NVIDIA plans to launch its first stand-alone CPU as part of the Vera Rubin platform in fall 2026.\\n    Quote: In fact, it's launching its first-ever stand-alone CPU this fall as part of the Vera Rubin platform.\",\n   \"- e61 [rumour; CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] NVIDIA’s current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are due to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including AWS, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\\n    Quote: Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e155; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e84 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source MarketScale 2026-07-06; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Vera Rubin rack first shipments are scheduled for Q3 2026, with volume ramping in Q4 2026.\\n    Quote: First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\",\n   \"- e107 [rumour; 247wallst.com (Danielle Liverance); original source Unnamed reports, as retold by 247wallst.com ; from gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06] Reports cited by the author said NVIDIA’s upcoming Vera Rubin server systems were delayed; the item gives no timing or details.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\",\n   \"- e155 [rumour; tikr.com; original source NVIDIA, as reported by tikr.com ; from gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250 2026-07-06] NVIDIA’s current-generation Rubin systems were reported to be in full production and scheduled to start shipping in fall 2026 to eight major cloud partners, including AWS, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud.\\n    Quote: That’s likely because the company’s current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and set to start shipping this fall to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e61; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e191 [fact; fool.com (Jack Delaney); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:9026c944c63575a080ce7ba9 2026-07-07] Nvidia announced in March 2026 that it plans to launch its Vera Rubin Space-1 Module; no launch date was given.\\n    Quote: Also, Nvidia announced in March that it will be launching its Vera Rubin Space-1 Module at some point in the future.\",\n   \"- e155 [rumour; tikr.com; original source NVIDIA, as reported by tikr.com ; from gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250 2026-07-06] NVIDIA’s current-generation Rubin systems were reported to be in full production and scheduled to start shipping in fall 2026 to eight major cloud partners, including AWS, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud.\\n    Quote: That’s likely because the company’s current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and set to start shipping this fall to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e61, e224; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e224 [third_party_view; straitstimes.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c 2026-07-08] NVIDIA is reported to be working toward releasing its Rubin product line in the second half of 2026.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is now working to release an even more powerful line-up called Rubin in the second half of 2026 .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e155; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e253 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source Bank of America ; from gnews:1b5a3359dd0644861c0fce42 2026-07-08] Bank of America estimated HBM content per rack would increase by about $200,000–$300,000 from Blackwell to Rubin, while total rack pricing would rise by $2 million–$3 million, from roughly $3 million–$4 million to $6 million–$7 million.\\n    Quote: The report estimates HBM content per rack could rise by about $0.2 million to $0.3 million from Blackwell to Rubin, while total rack pricing could increase by $2 million to $3 million, from roughly $3 million to $4 million for Blackwell to $6 million to $7 million for Vera Rubin.\",\n   \"- e389 [fact; barchart.com (author); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] The author reported that NVIDIA had recently launched its Vera Rubin AI platform and released its RTX Spark AI PC platform, expanded partnerships with Alphabet and Marvell, and invested further in networking, CPUs, and AI infrastructure.\\n    Quote: Nvidia recently launched its Vera Rubin AI platform, released the AI PC platform RTX Spark, expanded partnerships with companies such as Alphabet (GOOGL) and Marvell (MRVL), and further invested in networking, CPUs, and AI infrastructure.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e191 (); both are in force: Nvidia announced in March 2026 that it plans to launch its Vera Rubin Space-1 Module; no launch date was given.\",\n   \"- e390 [rumour; en.sedaily.com, citing the semiconductor industry; original source en.sedaily.com (unnamed-source report) 2026-07-12; from gnews:bf96def37a9c3a5f19f5d43c 2026-07-12] LG Electronics completed a Vera Rubin-specification AI server rack prototype in the first half of 2026 and plans to seek big-tech orders after second-half reliability evaluations, with mass production planned for 2027.\\n    Quote: According to the semiconductor industry on the 12th, LG Electronics' Production Research Institute (PRI) completed development of an AI server rack prototype matching the compute tray specifications of Nvidia's \\\"Vera Rubin\\\" in the first half of the year. LG plans to secure orders from global big tech companies after reliability evaluations in the second half and begin mass production next year.\",\n   \"- e23 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-02; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] The author says NVIDIA’s Vera Rubin platform is in full production and could begin shipping by the end of 2026.\\n    Quote: Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e501; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e473 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois) 2026-07-14; from gnews:ac8a489e54fbb1b85a1c7a2c 2026-07-14] The article said NVIDIA Blackwell 300 was ramping and Vera Rubin was queued to follow it.\\n    Quote: Blackwell 300 is ramping, and the Vera Rubin platform is queued behind it.\",\n   \"- e501 [third_party_view; EBC; original source EBC 2026-07-14; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] EBC reported that NVIDIA’s Vera Rubin architecture remained on track for shipment in late 2026.\\n    Quote: Blackwell Ultra and Rubin: Target shipping dates for Blackwell Ultra chips are ramping up fast and the Vera Rubin architecture remains officially on track for late 2026, but the market is currently priced for absolute perfection.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e23; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\"\n  ]\n }\n]\n[\n {\n  \"control\": 11,\n  \"ruler_question\": {\n   \"question\": \"What will Nvidia's total revenue be in FY2028?\",\n   \"unit\": \"USD\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2028-02-28\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Strongly bullish\",\n     \"condition\": \"FY2028 total revenue is at least $650 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 650000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Bullish\",\n     \"condition\": \"FY2028 total revenue is at least $550 billion and below $650 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 550000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": 650000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Neutral\",\n     \"condition\": \"FY2028 total revenue is at least $480 billion and below $550 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 480000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": 550000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Bearish\",\n     \"condition\": \"FY2028 total revenue is at least $420 billion and below $480 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 420000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": 480000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Strongly bearish\",\n     \"condition\": \"FY2028 total revenue is below $420 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": 420000000000.0\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"judged_question\": {\n   \"view_id\": \"v5\",\n   \"question\": \"What will NVIDIA report as total revenue for FY2028?\",\n   \"looks_at\": \"NVIDIA total revenue in FY2028\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2028-02-29\",\n   \"year\": \"FY2028\",\n   \"kind\": \"asset\"\n  },\n  \"judged_rungs\": [\n   {\n    \"position\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"Strongly bullish\",\n    \"condition\": \"FY2028 total revenue is at least $625 billion.\",\n    \"lo\": 625000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"Bullish\",\n    \"condition\": \"FY2028 total revenue is at least $554 billion and below $625 billion.\",\n    \"lo\": 554000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": 625000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 3,\n    \"label\": \"Neutral\",\n    \"condition\": \"FY2028 total revenue is at least $500 billion and below $554 billion, below the cited analyst estimate.\",\n    \"lo\": 500000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": 554000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 4,\n    \"label\": \"Bearish\",\n    \"condition\": \"FY2028 total revenue is at least $400 billion and below $500 billion.\",\n    \"lo\": 400000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": 500000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 5,\n    \"label\": \"Strongly bearish\",\n    \"condition\": \"FY2028 total revenue is below $400 billion.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": 400000000000.0\n   }\n  ],\n  \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i037\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-02\",\n    \"title\": \"Microsoft vs. Nvidia: Which Is the Better AI Stock to Own for the Next 3 Years?\",\n    \"passage\": \"from trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion to $392 billion this fiscal year, and $554 billion the following year\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"Wall Street analysts\",\n     \"what\": \"NVIDIA total revenue estimates\",\n     \"how_much\": \"$392 billion this fiscal year, $554 billion next year (from $253 billion trailing twelve months)\",\n     \"period\": \"this fiscal year (FY2027) and the following year (FY2028)\",\n     \"status\": \"forecast\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i256\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Is Down 17% From Its High. Is the Artificial Intelligence (AI) Leader Finally Cheap?\",\n    \"passage\": \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"big four AI hyperscalers' own plans (2026); NVIDIA's claim (2027)\",\n     \"what\": \"data-center capital expenditure of the big four\",\n     \"how_much\": \"around $650bn (2026); more than $1 trillion (2027)\",\n     \"period\": \"calendar 2026; calendar 2027\",\n     \"status\": \"plan (2026); forecast by NVIDIA (2027)\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i079\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-03\",\n    \"title\": \"Here's What a $25,000 Investment in Nvidia Could Be Worth if History Repeats Itself\",\n    \"passage\": \"In March, CEO Jensen Huang said he expects the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips to generate $1 trillion in sales between that time and the end of 2027\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"Jensen Huang\",\n     \"what\": \"Blackwell and Vera Rubin sales\",\n     \"how_much\": \"$1 trillion\",\n     \"period\": \"March 2026 to end-2027\",\n     \"status\": \"forecast\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i100\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n    \"passage\": \"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis (a research firm's report, not NVIDIA)\",\n     \"what\": \"Kyber NVL144 rack for Vera Rubin Ultra delayed; cause: PCB midplane manufacturability\",\n     \"how_much\": \"more than 12 months\",\n     \"period\": \"from 2027 to 2028\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\"\n    }\n   }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\": [\n   \"- e24 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] Wall Street analysts estimate NVIDIA revenue of $392 billion in FY2027 and $554 billion in FY2028, versus trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion.\\n    Quote: Wall Street analysts estimate that Nvidia's business could double over the next couple of years, from trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion to $392 billion this fiscal year, and $554 billion the following year.\",\n   \"- e32 [fact; Ben Wodecki, SDxCentral; original source Microsoft and MediaTek ; from gnews:1740f3733eedc34d8bac9b65 2026-07-03] Microsoft and MediaTek plan to launch microLED-based data-center networking interconnects for partners by late 2027, following a collaboration to develop Microsoft’s Mosaic prototype system.\\n    Quote: Those efforts were initially detailed in a paper published last September, but have since matured via a collaboration with Taiwanese fabless semiconductor company MediaTek. The pair plans to launch microLED-based data center networking interconnects for partners by late 2027 .\",\n   \"- e34 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, The Motley Fool, recounting Jensen Huang’s statement; original source Jensen Huang, NVIDIA CEO ; from gnews:e4e5f1b5c0c68381e4c486e1 2026-07-03] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang expected Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips to generate $1 trillion in sales from March 2026 through the end of 2027.\\n    Quote: In March, CEO Jensen Huang said he expects the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips to generate $1 trillion in sales between that time and the end of 2027.\",\n   \"- e35 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, The Motley Fool, recounting NVIDIA management’s statement; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:e4e5f1b5c0c68381e4c486e1 2026-07-03] NVIDIA announced plans in FY2027 Q1 to expand in CPUs, and management said it expected nearly $20 billion in CPU sales; the item gives no period for that sales estimate.\\n    Quote: Furthermore, in its first fiscal quarter of 2027, Nvidia announced plans to become much more involved in the central processing unit (CPU) space , which has been in high demand due to the rise of agentic AI. Management said they think they can generate nearly $20 billion of CPU sales, making it an instant industry leader.\",\n   \"- e36 [third_party_view; Bram Berkowitz, The Motley Fool, recounting Cerebras’ claim; original source Cerebras ; from gnews:e4e5f1b5c0c68381e4c486e1 2026-07-03] Cerebras claimed that its chips were 15 times faster than some NVIDIA GPU models.\\n    Quote: There are competitors like Cerebras and Space Exploration Technologies Corp , which have promised to make more powerful GPUs than Nvidia, and Cerebras already has chips it claims are 15 times faster than some of Nvidia's models.\",\n   \"- e24 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] Wall Street analysts estimate NVIDIA revenue of $392 billion in FY2027 and $554 billion in FY2028, versus trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion.\\n    Quote: Wall Street analysts estimate that Nvidia's business could double over the next couple of years, from trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion to $392 billion this fiscal year, and $554 billion the following year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e49; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e50 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] Wall Street analysts project NVIDIA revenue growth of 41% for FY2028.\\n    Quote: and 41% next year\",\n   \"- e97 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f 2026-07-06] Wall Street analysts expected NVIDIA growth of 41% in 2027.\\n    Quote: For 2027, that figure rises to 41%.\",\n   \"- e118 [party_statement; Futurum Group; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] Jensen Huang said the cost of a 1-gigawatt AI factory has risen from $20–30 billion to $50–60 billion and could reach $80–100 billion per gigawatt soon, with 100 gigawatts expected online before the end of the decade.\\n    Quote: Huang has said a 1-gigawatt AI factory that once cost $20–30 billion now runs $50–60 billion and will soon reach $80–100 billion per gigawatt, with 100 gigawatts expected online before the end of the decade.\",\n   \"- e166 [third_party_view; businesstimes.com.sg; original source Bloomberg Intelligence ; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] A Bloomberg Intelligence survey said Chinese executives expect domestic products’ share of their AI-accelerator budgets to rise to 46% over the next 12 months from 30% today.\\n    Quote: Executives in the country say they will allocate 46 per cent of their budget for AI accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months.\",\n   \"- e168 [third_party_view; businesstimes.com.sg; original source Bloomberg Intelligence survey respondents ; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] A large pool of respondents to the Bloomberg Intelligence survey was evaluating AI accelerators from Hygon Information Technology and Cambricon Technologies.\\n    Quote: AI accelerators produced by Hygon Information Technology and Cambricon Technologies were also being evaluated by a large pool of respondents to the survey.\",\n   \"- e169 [third_party_view; Bloomberg Intelligence report, quoted by businesstimes.com.sg; original source Bloomberg Intelligence ; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] Bloomberg Intelligence said China’s substitution of locally made AI semiconductors for foreign ones was making progress and was likely to benefit domestic makers such as Huawei and Hygon; its survey covered 60 executives.\\n    Quote: “China’s drive to substitute locally-made AI semiconductors for foreign ones is making progress, which is likely to benefit domestic makers such as Huawei and Hygon,” said the report, which surveyed 60 executives at Chinese software, finance, manufacturing and retail companies.\",\n   \"- e170 [third_party_view; businesstimes.com.sg; original source Bloomberg Intelligence ; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] A Bloomberg Intelligence survey expected Nvidia’s market share in China to shrink as H20 chips became harder to find and local firms took over, noting Beijing had urged Chinese technology firms not to use H20 chips.\\n    Quote: While Nvidia’s products remain popular, the Santa Clara-based firm’s market share is expected to shrink as its H20 chips, which Beijing has urged its tech firms not to use , become harder to find and local firms take over, according to the survey.\",\n   \"- e171 [fact; businesstimes.com.sg; original source China ; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] China is allocating roughly 2 trillion yuan (US$294 billion) to build data centers over the next five years as part of a government-led AI expansion initiative.\\n    Quote: China is allocating roughly two trillion yuan (US$294 billion) to build data centres across the country in the next five years, a government-led initiative to expand the use of AI in sectors from healthcare to city management.\",\n   \"- e172 [third_party_view; businesstimes.com.sg; original source Bloomberg News ; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] Bloomberg News reported that domestic companies would supply at least 80% of core technologies, including chips, for China’s data-center initiative.\\n    Quote: At least 80 per cent of core technologies, such as chips, will be supplied by domestic companies, Bloomberg News reported.\",\n   \"- e173 [third_party_view; businesstimes.com.sg; original source Bloomberg Intelligence report ; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] The report said a global memory-chip shortage was likely to cap growth at Chinese AI firms such as SMIC and that bottlenecks were shifting toward securing high-bandwidth memory supply.\\n    Quote: But a global memory chip shortage is likely to cap the growth of Chinese AI firms like Semiconductor Manufacturing International Corporation, with ChangXin Memory Technologies poised to reap the benefits.\",\n   \"- e186 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-07; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] The author says NVIDIA sales will likely accelerate as data-center spending shifts from construction costs to computing costs.\\n    Quote: There's a very strong correlation between increasing data center spending and Nvidia's sales, and that trend will likely accelerate as data centers shift from construction costs to computing costs.\",\n   \"- e187 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-07; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] The author expects Microsoft and TSMC to report strong earnings and forward guidance, which the author says could lift NVIDIA shares.\\n    Quote: I think both of these companies will report strong earnings with great forward guidance, and that could cause Nvidia's stock to rise.\",\n   \"- e188 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-07; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] The author says NVIDIA's core clients have not shifted their actual spending habits and expects NVIDIA to report a strong quarter in late August.\\n    Quote: Even if it doesn't, Nvidia reports earnings in late August and will likely report another blowout quarter, as there hasn't been any shift in actual spending habits from Nvidia's core clients yet.\",\n   \"- e190 [third_party_view; fool.com (Jack Delaney); original source SpaceX ; from gnews:9026c944c63575a080ce7ba9 2026-07-07] SpaceX plans to use Nvidia chips in its AI1 orbital data-center satellites, with Nvidia set to be an initial hardware supplier; no schedule or amount was given.\\n    Quote: That involvement with SpaceX will expand further, as Nvidia is set to serve as an initial supplier of hardware for SpaceX's orbital data centers. SpaceX's first version of the satellites it plans to use as data centers in space is called AI1 and will use Nvidia chips.\",\n   \"- e24 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] Wall Street analysts estimate NVIDIA revenue of $392 billion in FY2027 and $554 billion in FY2028, versus trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion.\\n    Quote: Wall Street analysts estimate that Nvidia's business could double over the next couple of years, from trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion to $392 billion this fiscal year, and $554 billion the following year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e49, e226; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e170 [third_party_view; businesstimes.com.sg; original source Bloomberg Intelligence ; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] A Bloomberg Intelligence survey expected Nvidia’s market share in China to shrink as H20 chips became harder to find and local firms took over, noting Beijing had urged Chinese technology firms not to use H20 chips.\\n    Quote: While Nvidia’s products remain popular, the Santa Clara-based firm’s market share is expected to shrink as its H20 chips, which Beijing has urged its tech firms not to use , become harder to find and local firms take over, according to the survey.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e243; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e172 [third_party_view; businesstimes.com.sg; original source Bloomberg News ; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] Bloomberg News reported that domestic companies would supply at least 80% of core technologies, including chips, for China’s data-center initiative.\\n    Quote: At least 80 per cent of core technologies, such as chips, will be supplied by domestic companies, Bloomberg News reported.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e244; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e233 [fact; Futuriom; original source Meta 2026-07-01; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] Bloomberg reported that Meta was setting up a cloud-based business to sell AI compute and models on July 1, 2026.\\n    Quote: On July 1, the same day NVIDIA announced its new revenue-sharing model, Bloomberg reported that Meta is setting up a cloud-based business selling AI compute and models.\",\n   \"- e34 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, The Motley Fool, recounting Jensen Huang’s statement; original source Jensen Huang, NVIDIA CEO ; from gnews:e4e5f1b5c0c68381e4c486e1 2026-07-03] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang expected Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips to generate $1 trillion in sales from March 2026 through the end of 2027.\\n    Quote: In March, CEO Jensen Huang said he expects the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips to generate $1 trillion in sales between that time and the end of 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e281; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e281 [party_statement; fool.com (Adam Spatacco); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:931ee0196ca609e637235fad 2026-07-09] NVIDIA management cited roughly $1 trillion of revenue visibility for Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear customer commitments.\\n    Quote: Management has also articulated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e34; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e287 [third_party_view; QZ.com; original source Bloomberg Intelligence ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Bloomberg Intelligence projects NVIDIA will hold 70%–75% of the AI chip market through 2030.\\n    Quote: Bloomberg Intelligence projects the company will hold 70% to 75% of the AI chip market through 2030.\",\n   \"- e290 [third_party_view; QZ.com; original source Meta ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Meta plans to build three more MTIA chip generations over the next two years to run generative AI models.\\n    Quote: Meta is set to build three more generations over the next two years with the purpose of running generative AI models.\",\n   \"- e291 [third_party_view; QZ.com; original source QZ.com 2026-07-09; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Meta's MTIA 450 is expected to arrive in early 2027.\\n    Quote: The next one, MTIA 450, arrives in early 2027, doubling how fast the chip moves data in and out of memory.\",\n   \"- e292 [third_party_view; QZ.com; original source QZ.com 2026-07-09; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Meta's MTIA 500 is expected to arrive later in 2027.\\n    Quote: Its eventual successor, MTIA 500, is expected to come later next year, and pushes memory speed another 50% higher.\",\n   \"- e294 [third_party_view; QZ.com; original source Reuters ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Tesla completed the design of its AI5 inference chip in April 2026 and plans to produce it at TSMC's Arizona plant and Samsung's Texas plant.\\n    Quote: Tesla finished the design of its AI5 inference chip in April 2026, according to Reuters . Production is planned at both TSMC 's plant in Arizona and Samsung's plant in Texas.\",\n   \"- e295 [party_statement; QZ.com; original source Tesla ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Tesla is targeting mid-2027 to begin mass production of its AI5 chip.\\n    Quote: Tesla is targeting mid-2027 for mass production.\",\n   \"- e302 [party_statement; au.finance.yahoo.com; original source Cerebras ; from gnews:66cb0387229ac4b1390615cb 2026-07-09] Cerebras plans to build a European AI data-centre network with 200 MW of combined power capacity by the end of 2027, expanding compute capacity in a market served by NVIDIA.\\n    Quote: The company plans to build a network of AI data centres across Europe by the end of 2027, with a combined power capacity of 200 MW.\",\n   \"- e303 [party_statement; Andrew Feldman, quoted by au.finance.yahoo.com; original source Andrew Feldman, Cerebras CEO ; from gnews:66cb0387229ac4b1390615cb 2026-07-09] Cerebras CEO Andrew Feldman said the company’s European AI data-centre expansions are worth several billion dollars.\\n    Quote: \\\"These are massive expansions\\\" worth several billion dollars, chief executive Andrew Feldman told AFP on the sidelines of the RAISE Summit in Paris.\",\n   \"- e306 [third_party_view; Trefis analyst; original source Wall Street analyst consensus ; from gnews:6abaf2856736877236d30f6a 2026-07-10] Trefis reported analyst consensus forecasts NVIDIA revenue growth of about 32% annually over the next few years.\\n    Quote: Wall Street consensus sees NVIDIA’s revenue growing about 32% a year for the next few years.\",\n   \"- e170 [third_party_view; businesstimes.com.sg; original source Bloomberg Intelligence ; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] A Bloomberg Intelligence survey expected Nvidia’s market share in China to shrink as H20 chips became harder to find and local firms took over, noting Beijing had urged Chinese technology firms not to use H20 chips.\\n    Quote: While Nvidia’s products remain popular, the Santa Clara-based firm’s market share is expected to shrink as its H20 chips, which Beijing has urged its tech firms not to use , become harder to find and local firms take over, according to the survey.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e243, e386; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e339 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Silicon Analysts ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] Silicon Analysts estimated that NVIDIA held more than 90% of the AI training-chip market in 2025.\\n    Quote: According to Silicon Analysts , which compiles estimates from TrendForce, Morgan Stanley $MS , and TSMC $TSM capacity data, Nvidia 's share exceeded 90% in training in 2025.\",\n   \"- e340 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Silicon Analysts ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] Silicon Analysts estimated NVIDIA's AI inference-chip market share at 60%–75% in 2025, amid competition from custom silicon.\\n    Quote: Its inference share sat between 60% and 75% over the same period, undercut by growing competition from custom silicon.\",\n   \"- e341 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source TechCrunch ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] TechCrunch reported that 1.4 million Amazon Trainium chips were deployed across three generations as of March, including more than one million Trainium2 chips running Anthropic's Claude.\\n    Quote: As of March, 1.4 million Trainium chips were deployed across three generations, according to TechCrunch , with Anthropic's Claude running on more than one million Trainium2 chips alone.\",\n   \"- e342 [fact; qz.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] Amazon committed 2 GW of Trainium computing capacity to OpenAI.\\n    Quote: Amazon has also committed two gigawatts of Trainium computing capacity to OpenAI, putting its chips inside two of the industry's most prominent AI labs.\",\n   \"- e343 [fact; qz.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] AMD launched ROCm 7 and a free developer cloud.\\n    Quote: AMD launched ROCm 7 and a free developer cloud.\",\n   \"- e344 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Mizuho Securities and Silicon Analysts ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] Mizuho Securities estimated NVIDIA's overall AI-chip market share at 70%–95% depending on segment; Silicon Analysts projected it would settle near 75% as the total market grows beyond $200 billion.\\n    Quote: Nvidia 's overall AI chip market share, estimated by Mizuho Securities at between 70% and 95% depending on segment, is projected by Silicon Analysts to settle near 75% as the total market expands past $200 billion.\",\n   \"- e348 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Broadcom ; from gnews:f8f440b5020fec9773812b6e 2026-07-12] Broadcom plans to ramp production of custom AI chips for Meta Platforms, Anthropic, and OpenAI starting late in 2026 and continuing into 2027.\\n    Quote: However, Broadcom is also making custom AI chips for Meta Platforms , Anthropic, and OpenAI, and production for those chips is scheduled to ramp up late this year and into next.\",\n   \"- e349 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-12; from gnews:f8f440b5020fec9773812b6e 2026-07-12] The author says the market expects NVIDIA to maintain its lead in growth for the full fiscal year, while Broadcom narrows the gap.\\n    Quote: Looking ahead, the market still expects Nvidia to maintain its lead for the full fiscal year, though Broadcom is starting to close the gap.\",\n   \"- e359 [party_statement; tech-insider.org, reporting NVIDIA’s statement; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] NVIDIA said AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs in 2027–2028.\\n    Quote: The same AI boom that has made Nvidia one of the most valuable companies on the planet is now directly limiting what it can sell to the gamers who built its original fanbase. Every HBM stack that goes into a data center accelerator is capacity that does not go into a GeForce card, and Nvidia said in August 2026 that AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional Nvidia GPUs in 2027 to 2028 .\",\n   \"- e385 [third_party_view; barchart.com (author), citing a Bloomberg survey; original source Bloomberg survey respondents ; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] A Bloomberg survey found Chinese technology companies expect domestic suppliers to receive about 46% of their AI-chip spending over the next year, up from about 30% currently.\\n    Quote: A new Bloomberg survey showed Chinese technology companies expect to allocate roughly 46% of their AI chip spending to domestic suppliers over the next year, up from about 30% currently.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e166; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e386 [third_party_view; barchart.com (author); original source barchart.com (author) 2026-07-12; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] The author said Beijing has encouraged companies to reduce purchases of foreign AI processors, adding pressure to NVIDIA’s long-term growth in China.\\n    Quote: Beijing has also encouraged companies to reduce purchases of foreign AI processors, creating additional pressure on Nvidia's long-term growth in one of its largest overseas markets.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e170; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e393 [party_statement; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dedacecd6d2ed5192c307199 2026-07-13] NVIDIA expects sovereign AI projects and on-premise deployments to account for a larger share of future growth; no period was specified.\\n    Quote: It expects sovereign AI projects and on-premise deployments to make up a larger share of future growth as AI adoption expands.\",\n   \"- e395 [third_party_view; fool.com (Sean Williams); original source Sean Williams 2026-07-13; from gnews:e7e8cbf33738ceb5865837f0 2026-07-13] The author argues that GPU supply shortages support NVIDIA’s pricing power and gross margin, and that customer-developed chips occupying data-center capacity could erode that advantage.\\n    Quote: For starters, some of Nvidia's pricing power and otherworldly gross margin originates from persistent GPU supply shortages. If the company's top customers are developing chips that take up valuable data center real estate, Nvidia can quickly lose its biggest tailwind .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e267; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e396 [third_party_view; fool.com (Sean Williams); original source Sean Williams 2026-07-13; from gnews:e7e8cbf33738ceb5865837f0 2026-07-13] The author says hyperscalers’ internally developed AI chips are less costly and presumably more available than NVIDIA GPUs, whose demand has created a sizable backlog.\\n    Quote: Secondly, these internally developed chips are notably less costly than Nvidia's hardware and, presumably, more readily available (a function of demand creating a sizable backlog for Nvidia's GPUs).\",\n   \"- e397 [third_party_view; fool.com (Sean Williams); original source Sean Williams 2026-07-13; from gnews:e7e8cbf33738ceb5865837f0 2026-07-13] The author argues that cost-effective in-house AI chips at NVIDIA’s top customers may delay hyperscalers’ data-center upgrades and future NVIDIA GPU purchases.\\n    Quote: Lastly, AI chips developed by Nvidia's top customers may delay future upgrade cycles. Between Nvidia's accelerated innovation cycle, which is depreciating prior-generation GPUs, and the company's top customers developing cost-effective in-house solutions, hyperscalers may push out AI data center upgrades.\",\n   \"- e400 [fact; gurufocus.com; original source Google and Blackstone ; from gnews:1c47fd978ef50b1d7f48cbd8 2026-07-13] Google and Blackstone are working on a cloud venture that plans to rent TPU capacity to AI labs and financial firms in 2027, potentially adding competition for NVIDIA cloud demand.\\n    Quote: Google is also working with Blackstone on a new cloud venture that plans to rent TPU capacity to AI labs and financial firms next year.\",\n   \"- e421 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source Meta ; from gnews:221f52333c8f5e936e515c4d 2026-07-13] Meta raised its 2026 capital expenditure plan to $125–145 billion and launched Meta Compute, a service to rent excess internal AI capacity to enterprises.\\n    Quote: Meta raised 2026 capex to $125 to $145 billion, then quietly launched Meta Compute, a service renting its excess internal AI capacity to external enterprises.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e233 (2026-07-01); both are in force: Bloomberg reported that Meta was setting up a cloud-based business to sell AI compute and models on July 1, 2026.\",\n   \"- e425 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Morgan Stanley said hyperscalers continue expanding proprietary-silicon investment to lower total cost of ownership and reduce reliance on merchant GPU suppliers.\\n    Quote: Morgan Stanley says hyperscalers continue to expand their investments in proprietary silicon to improve total cost of ownership and reduce dependence on merchant GPU suppliers.\",\n   \"- e427 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com; original source Milk Road AI ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Milk Road AI estimated that one gigawatt of AI infrastructure costs $6 billion–$11 billion with Broadcom- or Marvell-designed ASIC racks, versus roughly $19 billion with Nvidia GB300-based racks.\\n    Quote: Milk Road AI estimates that building one gigawatt of AI infrastructure using Broadcom- or Marvell-designed ASIC racks cost between $6 billion and $11 billion, compared with roughly $19 billion using Nvidia GB300-based racks.\",\n   \"- e428 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com; original source Milk Road AI ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Milk Road AI estimated that rack costs using Vera Rubin would be $25 billion.\\n    Quote: Under Vera Rubin, rack costs jump to $25 billion.\",\n   \"- e24 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] Wall Street analysts estimate NVIDIA revenue of $392 billion in FY2027 and $554 billion in FY2028, versus trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion.\\n    Quote: Wall Street analysts estimate that Nvidia's business could double over the next couple of years, from trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion to $392 billion this fiscal year, and $554 billion the following year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e49, e226, e437, e438; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e281 [party_statement; fool.com (Adam Spatacco); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:931ee0196ca609e637235fad 2026-07-09] NVIDIA management cited roughly $1 trillion of revenue visibility for Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear customer commitments.\\n    Quote: Management has also articulated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e34, e436; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e344 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Mizuho Securities and Silicon Analysts ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] Mizuho Securities estimated NVIDIA's overall AI-chip market share at 70%–95% depending on segment; Silicon Analysts projected it would settle near 75% as the total market grows beyond $200 billion.\\n    Quote: Nvidia 's overall AI chip market share, estimated by Mizuho Securities at between 70% and 95% depending on segment, is projected by Silicon Analysts to settle near 75% as the total market expands past $200 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e444; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e421 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source Meta ; from gnews:221f52333c8f5e936e515c4d 2026-07-13] Meta raised its 2026 capital expenditure plan to $125–145 billion and launched Meta Compute, a service to rent excess internal AI capacity to enterprises.\\n    Quote: Meta raised 2026 capex to $125 to $145 billion, then quietly launched Meta Compute, a service renting its excess internal AI capacity to external enterprises.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e460; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e233 (2026-07-01); both are in force: Bloomberg reported that Meta was setting up a cloud-based business to sell AI compute and models on July 1, 2026.\",\n   \"- e436 [party_statement; fool.com author Justin Pope; original source NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang ; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said he sees at least $1 trillion in revenue from the Blackwell and Rubin platforms through the end of calendar 2027.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, CEO Jensen Huang has said that he sees at least $1 trillion in revenue from Nvidia's Blackwell and Rubin platforms through the end of 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e281; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e437 [third_party_view; fool.com author Justin Pope; original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] Wall Street analysts estimate NVIDIA revenue of approximately $555 billion for FY2028, ending January 2028.\\n    Quote: Wall Street analysts estimate that Nvidia will generate approximately $555 billion in revenue for the company's next fiscal year, ending January 2028.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e24; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e438 [third_party_view; fool.com author Justin Pope; original source fool.com author Justin Pope 2026-07-14; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] The author cites NVIDIA trailing 12-month revenue of $253 billion as the base for a forecast that sales could roughly double within two years.\\n    Quote: In other words, sales could roughly double within the next two years, based on Nvidia's trailing 12-month revenue of $253 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e24; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e439 [party_statement; The Motley Fool author, reporting TSMC's guidance; original source TSMC ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] TSMC raised its forecast for global semiconductor-market revenue in 2030 from $1 trillion to $1.5 trillion, citing AI-fueled chip demand.\\n    Quote: Nvidia's foundry partner TSMC recently noted that the global semiconductor market's revenue could reach a whopping $1.5 trillion in 2030. The Taiwan-based foundry giant had previously anticipated $1 trillion in semiconductor revenue by the end of the decade. However, AI-fueled demand for chips led to a substantial upgrade to its guidance.\",\n   \"- e440 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author, reporting TSMC's estimate; original source TSMC ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] TSMC expects AI and high-performance-computing chips to represent 55% of the projected 2030 global semiconductor-market opportunity.\\n    Quote: TSMC points out that AI and high-performance computing (HPC) chips will account for 55% of this lucrative opportunity.\",\n   \"- e441 [fact; The Motley Fool author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported fiscal 2026 data-center revenue of $193.7 billion.\\n    Quote: For comparison, Nvidia's data center revenue in fiscal 2026 (which ended in January this year) was $193.7 billion.\",\n   \"- e442 [fact; The Motley Fool author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] NVIDIA's fiscal 2026 data-center revenue included $162.3 billion from compute-chip sales, with the remainder from networking components.\\n    Quote: It is worth noting that $162.3 billion of its fiscal 2026 data center revenue came from sales of compute chips, while the rest was from networking components.\",\n   \"- e443 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author; original source Analysts ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] Analysts believe NVIDIA's share of the AI data-center chip market may have peaked.\\n    Quote: However, analysts believe that Nvidia's AI data center chip market share may have peaked.\",\n   \"- e444 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author, reporting analysts' estimate; original source Analysts ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] Analysts anticipate NVIDIA's AI-chip market share will decline to 75% in 2026.\\n    Quote: That's why Nvidia's AI chip market share is anticipated to decline to 75% this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e344; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e445 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author; original source The Motley Fool author 2026-07-14; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] The author estimates that NVIDIA could generate more than $400 billion in 2030 data-center chip revenue if it has 50% market share of an $825 billion market.\\n    Quote: Let's assume Nvidia continues to lose ground in AI chips for the next four years and ends up at just 50% market share in 2030; it can still generate more than $400 billion in data center chip revenue in 2030 (based on the $825 billion market size estimated above).\",\n   \"- e446 [fact; The Motley Fool author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported fiscal 2026 networking revenue of $31.4 billion, up 142% year over year.\\n    Quote: The company reported a 142% year-over-year increase in networking revenue in fiscal 2026 to $31.4 billion.\",\n   \"- e447 [fact; The Motley Fool author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 first-quarter networking revenue of $14.8 billion, triple the year-earlier amount.\\n    Quote: It has started fiscal 2027 on a stronger note in this segment, with networking revenue tripling year-over-year to $14.8 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e272; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e448 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author, reporting Mordor Intelligence's forecast; original source Mordor Intelligence ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] Mordor Intelligence expects the InfiniBand market to grow 36% annually over the next five years and exceed $164 billion in revenue in 2031.\\n    Quote: The InfiniBand market, for instance, is expected to clock 36% annual growth over the next five years, according to Mordor Intelligence. It could generate more than $164 billion in revenue in 2031.\",\n   \"- e449 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author, reporting Dell'Oro Group's forecast; original source Dell'Oro Group ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] Dell'Oro Group projects that the data-center switch market will exceed $100 billion in revenue by 2030.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, the data center switch market is projected to exceed $100 billion in revenue by 2030, according to Dell'Oro Group.\",\n   \"- e450 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author; original source The Motley Fool author 2026-07-14; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] The author estimates NVIDIA's combined data-center compute and networking addressable opportunity could exceed $1 trillion by 2030.\\n    Quote: In all, Nvidia's data center addressable opportunity, including both networking and compute, could surpass $1 trillion by the end of the decade.\",\n   \"- e451 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author; original source The Motley Fool author 2026-07-14; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] The author says consensus revenue-growth projections for NVIDIA through fiscal 2029 have increased significantly.\\n    Quote: That's why there has been a significant jump in Nvidia's consensus revenue growth projections through fiscal 2029.\",\n   \"- e453 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source TD Cowen analyst Joshua Buchalter 2026-07-08; from gnews:a0b0f251bbab383483ec679b 2026-07-14] TD Cowen analyst Joshua Buchalter said NVIDIA’s integrated hardware and software platform gives it an underappreciated competitive edge in the next AI phase, that AI spending is durable across applications and customers, and that industry-specific AI deployments will become a much larger growth driver over time.\\n    Quote: The analyst opines that NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) possesses an underappreciated competitive edge in the next phase of AI. This is because it provides an integrated hardware and software platform, which works seamlessly together. The analyst remains confident that AI spending is durable throughout different applications and customers.\",\n   \"- e506 [party_statement; Jakarta Globe; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:e001119c6342373f6b1f3cf4 2026-07-14] Firmus plans to deploy about 170,000 of NVIDIA’s latest-generation GPUs in phases from Q1 2027 through early 2028 to provide AI computing capacity.\\n    Quote: Under the partnership, Firmus plans to deploy about 170,000 of Nvidia's latest-generation graphics processing units (GPUs) in phases from the first quarter of 2027 through early 2028, providing AI computing capacity for startups and fast-growing AI companies.\",\n   \"- e507 [third_party_view; Jakarta Globe, citing The Straits Times; original source The Straits Times ; from gnews:e001119c6342373f6b1f3cf4 2026-07-14] The Straits Times reported that the Batam data center project is projected to generate up to $30 billion in off-take agreements during its first six years of operation.\\n    Quote: According to The Straits Times , the project is projected to generate as much as $30 billion in off-take agreements during its first six years of operation.\"\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"control\": 12,\n  \"ruler_question\": {\n   \"question\": \"How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?\",\n   \"unit\": \"USD\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-12-31\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Below $550 billion\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is less than $550 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": 550000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"labe