dar 2027\", \"status\": \"plan (2026); forecast by NVIDIA (2027)\"}}\nEVIDENCE [\n  \"- e48 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] NVIDIA projects that the four largest AI hyperscalers’ combined data-center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027.\\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\",\n  \"- e48 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] NVIDIA projects that the four largest AI hyperscalers’ combined data-center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027.\\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e94; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n  \"- e94 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f 2026-07-06] NVIDIA management forecast that hyperscaler spending would exceed $1 trillion in 2027.\\n    Quote: During its last quarterly conference call, management forecast that it expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e48; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n  \"- e94 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f 2026-07-06] NVIDIA management forecast that hyperscaler spending would exceed $1 trillion in 2027.\\n    Quote: During its last quarterly conference call, management forecast that it expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e48, e185; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n  \"- e185 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] NVIDIA claims the four largest AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027.\\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e94, e193; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n  \"- e193 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-07; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] The author estimated AI hyperscalers’ data-center capital expenditures could exceed $1 trillion in calendar 2027.\\n    Quote: next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e185; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n  \"- e185 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] NVIDIA claims the four largest AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027.\\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e94, e193, e347; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n  \"- e347 [party_statement; fool.com analyst; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:07cf5835d106467db5448c13 2026-07-12] NVIDIA told investors it expects AI hyperaccelerator spending to exceed $1 trillion next year; the item does not specify the spending population or calendar/fiscal basis.\\n    Quote: However, Nvidia told investors last quarter that it expects AI hyperaccelerator spending to rise to over $1 trillion next year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e185; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n  \"- e193 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-07; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] The author estimated AI hyperscalers’ data-center capital expenditures could exceed $1 trillion in calendar 2027.\\n    Quote: next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e185, e426; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n  \"- e426 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Morgan Stanley forecast that Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and Meta will spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure and add 19.5 GW of compute capacity in calendar 2027; Google is forecast to add 6.8 GW. The article says the four added roughly 6.7 GW in 2025.\\n    Quote: The analyst says Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and Meta are forecast to spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure next year, adding 19.5 gigawatts (GW) of incremental compute capacity. In 2025, they added roughly 6.7 GW. Google alone will add 6.8 GW, or more than the hyperscalers did combined two years ago.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e193; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n  \"- e472 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois), quoting Jensen Huang; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:ac8a489e54fbb1b85a1c7a2c 2026-07-14] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said the AI-factory buildout was accelerating at extraordinary speed.\\n    Quote: Jensen Huang framed the moment bluntly: “The buildout of AI factories, the largest infrastructure expansion in human history, is accelerating at extraordinary speed.”\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e481; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n  \"- e481 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja), quoting Jensen Huang; original source Jensen Huang, NVIDIA CEO ; from gnews:184645c8d683d95b684bd6da 2026-07-14] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said AI factory buildout was accelerating at extraordinary speed.\\n    Quote: “The buildout of AI factories , the largest infrastructure expansion in human history, is accelerating at extraordinary speed.”\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e472; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\"\n]\n\nPOINT {'control': 15, 'field': 'item i309'}\nITEM {\"id\": \"i309\", \"day\": \"2026-07-14\", \"title\": \"Nvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington\", \"passage\": \"President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"US President Trump\", \"what\": \"allowed H200 exports to select customers in China\", \"period\": \"December 2025\", \"status\": \"happened\"}}\nEVIDENCE [\n  \"- e222 [rumour; straitstimes.com; original source The Information 2026-07-08; from gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c 2026-07-08] The Information reported on July 8, citing unnamed sources, that Chinese officials told Alibaba, ByteDance and DeepSeek they could seek approval to buy limited quantities of NVIDIA H200 chips, subject to stating the quantity and reason for purchase.\\n    Quote: China plans to allow top artificial intelligence companies to buy a limited amount of H200 chips from Nvidia, a sign the country is easing restrictions on the coveted US technology, according to the Information news site. Chinese officials have informed companies such as Alibaba Group, ByteDance and DeepSeek that they will have permission to purchase some of the processors, which are used to devel\",\n  \"- e223 [rumour; straitstimes.com; original source The Information 2026-07-08; from gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c 2026-07-08] The Information reported on July 8, citing unnamed sources, that China was still determining the permitted H200 purchase quantity and the total could be fewer than 200,000 chips.\\n    Quote: The Chinese government, meanwhile, is still figuring out how many H200 chips to allow the companies to buy, but the total may end up being fewer than 200,000, the Information reported.\",\n  \"- e222 [rumour; straitstimes.com; original source The Information 2026-07-08; from gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c 2026-07-08] The Information reported on July 8, citing unnamed sources, that Chinese officials told Alibaba, ByteDance and DeepSeek they could seek approval to buy limited quantities of NVIDIA H200 chips, subject to stating the quantity and reason for purchase.\\n    Quote: China plans to allow top artificial intelligence companies to buy a limited amount of H200 chips from Nvidia, a sign the country is easing restrictions on the coveted US technology, according to the Information news site. Chinese officials have informed companies such as Alibaba Group, ByteDance and DeepSeek that they will have permission to purchase some of the processors, which are used to devel\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e284, e301; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n  \"- e284 [rumour; technologyreview.com; original source China ; from gnews:50a84fa6b2f3eb33f29948a4 2026-07-09] China reportedly plans to permit Alibaba, ByteDance and DeepSeek to buy NVIDIA H200 chips; no date or quantity was given.\\n    Quote: China plans to let its top AI firms buy Nvidia H200 chips\",\n  \"- e285 [rumour; South China Morning Post; original source An unnamed source with knowledge of the matter ; from gnews:91684b863d274b10246d0c13 2026-07-09] An unnamed source said China’s government plans to let selected companies, including Alibaba, buy limited numbers of NVIDIA H200 chips; no date or quantity was specified.\\n    Quote: Now, however, China’s stance is beginning to change, as the government plans to let selected companies – including Alibaba Group Holding – buy limited numbers of the device, a source with knowledge of the matter told the South China Morning Post.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e284; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n  \"- e301 [fact; moomoo.com; original source China ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] China is moving to grant limited approval for domestic firms to buy NVIDIA H200 processors, with firms required to seek individual approval for quantities and use cases.\\n    Quote: The stock is also navigating a complex geopolitical environment as China moves to grant limited approval for domestic firms to purchase H200 processors. While this policy shift eases access to Nvidia’s technology, companies must now request individual approval for specific quantities and use cases.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e222; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n  \"- e320 [third_party_view; David Moadel / 24/7 Wall St.; original source NVIDIA management (outlook assumption); David Moadel / 24/7 Wall St. (upside interpretation) ; from gnews:cbb9ff1a37ebb451f9321da5 2026-07-10] NVIDIA's Q2 FY2027 revenue outlook assumed zero Data Center compute revenue from China; the author says a policy change permitting H200 exports could add upside.\\n    Quote: Notably, that outlook assumed zero Data Center compute revenue from China, meaning any policy thaw on H200 exports could represent additional upside.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e313; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n  \"- e320 [third_party_view; David Moadel / 24/7 Wall St.; original source NVIDIA management (outlook assumption); David Moadel / 24/7 Wall St. (upside interpretation) ; from gnews:cbb9ff1a37ebb451f9321da5 2026-07-10] NVIDIA's Q2 FY2027 revenue outlook assumed zero Data Center compute revenue from China; the author says a policy change permitting H200 exports could add upside.\\n    Quote: Notably, that outlook assumed zero Data Center compute revenue from China, meaning any policy thaw on H200 exports could represent additional upside.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e313, e410; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n  \"- e430 [party_statement; Jeffery Kessler; original source Jeffery Kessler, Under Secretary of Commerce for Industry and Security 2026-07-14; from gnews:9d898f2bf43ed52a98e1587a 2026-07-14] Under Secretary of Commerce for Industry and Security Jeffery Kessler said that very few NVIDIA H200 and equivalent chips had shipped to China and Hong Kong under licenses; he gave no shipment amount or period.\\n    Quote: The bottom line is very few shipments against licenses for H200s and equivalents have taken place. It's a very small quantity of chips,\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e452, e499; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n  \"- e431 [party_statement; Jeffery Kessler; original source Jeffery Kessler, Under Secretary of Commerce for Industry and Security 2026-07-14; from gnews:9d898f2bf43ed52a98e1587a 2026-07-14] Jeffery Kessler said the U.S. government assesses H200 license applications case by case, requiring applicants to meet national security requirements and submit to compliance inspections.\\n    Quote: Kessler said that the U.S. government assessed companies that want the H200 chips on a case-by-case basis, with applicants needing to meet national security requirements and submit to inspections to make sure the chips are compliant.\",\n  \"- e432 [party_statement; Jeffery Kessler; original source Jeffery Kessler, Under Secretary of Commerce for Industry and Security 2026-07-14; from gnews:9d898f2bf43ed52a98e1587a 2026-07-14] Jeffery Kessler said the U.S. government denies some applications for H200 export licenses; he gave no number of denials.\\n    Quote: There are cases where we deny the license applications we receive,\",\n  \"- e452 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source A top U.S. commerce official ; from gnews:c73b90b63a733c6ab712fcdd 2026-07-14] A top U.S. commerce official told Congress that NVIDIA H200 chip shipments to China had begun, with very few shipped so far.\\n    Quote: Separately, a top U.S. commerce official told Congress that while the number of H200 chips shipped to China so far was \\\"very few,\\\" the shipments have commenced.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e430; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n  \"- e468 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source Beijing ; from gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb 2026-07-14] China has refused to allow Chinese companies to buy NVIDIA H200 GPUs and is relying on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to fill the gap.\\n    Quote: However, Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy these AI processors — instead, it’s banking on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to make up for the shortfall, but it’s apparently still not enough.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e223 (2026-07-08); both are in force: The Information reported on July 8, citing unnamed sources, that China was still determining the permitted H200 purchase quantity and the total could be fewer than 200,000 chips.\",\n  \"- e486 [fact; Reuters; original source U.S. authorities ; from gnews:24268bfaab6ef1d7876b188c 2026-07-14] The U.S. permitted ZTE Kangxun Telecom and server maker Maginfra to purchase NVIDIA H200 chips; the item gives no approval date or purchase amount.\\n    Quote: ZTE Kangxun Telecom and server maker Maginfra have been permitted to purchase Nvidia's H200 chips\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e497; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n  \"- e487 [rumour; Reuters; original source Unnamed sources familiar with the matter ; from gnews:24268bfaab6ef1d7876b188c 2026-07-14] Unnamed sources said some Chinese cloud firms recently told partners and clients they may soon be able to obtain H200 chips, indicating progress in Chinese authorities' import reviews.\\n    Quote: some Chinese cloud firms have recently told partners and clients they may soon be able to obtain H200 chips, the sources said, indicating some progress in import reviews by Chinese authorities.\",\n  \"- e497 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source The U.S. government 2026-07-14; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] The U.S. approved ZTE and Maginfra to buy NVIDIA H200 chips; the item reports the approval on 2026-07-14.\\n    Quote: Today, Reuters reports that Chinese telecoms giant ZTE and server firm Maginfra have received U.S. approval to buy Nvidia's last-gen H200 \\\"Hopper\\\" chips.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e486; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n  \"- e498 [third_party_view; Tom's Hardware, recounting Reuters; original source Reuters ; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] Reuters reported that Chinese technology firms had more than two million NVIDIA H200 chips on order about six months before 2026-07-14, exceeding NVIDIA's available supply at the time.\\n    Quote: But even with those domestic production initiatives, the Chinese hunger for AI silicon is so deep that six months ago, Reuters said the nation's tech firms had more than two million H200 chips on order, far more than what Nvidia had on hand at the time.\",\n  \"- e499 [party_statement; Tom's Hardware, quoting CNBC's account of a U.S. trade official's remarks; original source A U.S. trade official 2026-07-14; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] A U.S. trade official said that very few H200 and equivalent chips had shipped against licenses, describing the quantity as very small, on 2026-07-14.\\n    Quote: CNBC cites a U.S. trade official who today stated that \\\"very few shipments against licenses for H200s and equivalents have taken place. It’s a very small quantity of chips\\\" during a congressional hearing.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e430; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\"\n]\n\nPOINT {'control': 16, 'field': 'same_question'}\n{\n  \"ruler_question\": {\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a guarantee of OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease by 2027-06-30?\",\n    \"unit\": \"yes/no\",\n    \"due_day\": \"2027-06-30\",\n    \"rungs\": [\n      {\n        \"label\": \"Yes\",\n        \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has signed the guarantee by the due day.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"label\": \"No\",\n        \"condition\": \"It has not.\"\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"judged_question\": {\n    \"view_id\": \"v2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee for OpenAI’s lease of the large Ohio data center by 2027-07-01?\",\n    \"looks_at\": \"NVIDIA guarantee for OpenAI’s Ohio data-center lease\",\n    \"due_day\": \"2027-07-01\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\",\n    \"kind\": \"asset\"\n  },\n  \"judged_rungs\": [\n    {\n      \"position\": 1,\n      \"label\": \"Yes\",\n      \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has signed a financial guarantee for OpenAI’s Ohio data-center lease by 2027-07-01.\",\n      \"lo\": null,\n      \"hi\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"position\": 2,\n      \"label\": \"No\",\n      \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has not signed a financial guarantee for OpenAI’s Ohio data-center lease by 2027-07-01.\",\n      \"lo\": null,\n      \"hi\": null\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\nPOINT {'control': 22, 'field': 'item i309'}\nITEM {\"id\": \"i309\", \"day\": \"2026-07-14\", \"title\": \"Nvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington\", \"passage\": \"President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"US President Trump\", \"what\": \"allowed H200 exports to select customers in China\", \"period\": \"December 2025\", \"status\": \"happened\"}}\nEVIDENCE [\n  \"- e203 [rumour; straitstimes.com; original source The Information 2026-07-08; from gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c 2026-07-08] Chinese officials reportedly told Alibaba, ByteDance and DeepSeek they would be permitted to purchase some Nvidia H200 chips; the report was published July 8, 2026.\\n    Quote: Chinese officials have informed companies such as Alibaba Group, ByteDance and DeepSeek that they will have permission to purchase some of the processors, which are used to develop AI models, the news site said on July 8 .\",\n  \"- e204 [rumour; straitstimes.com; original source The Information 2026-07-08; from gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c 2026-07-08] The Information reported that China was still determining the number of H200 chips firms could buy, with the total potentially below 200,000.\\n    Quote: The Chinese government, meanwhile, is still figuring out how many H200 chips to allow the companies to buy, but the total may end up being fewer than 200,000, the Information reported.\",\n  \"- e206 [party_statement; straitstimes.com; original source Nvidia CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c 2026-07-08] Nvidia CFO Colette Kress said in May 2026 that the company was uncertain whether China would allow any H200 imports.\\n    Quote: “We are uncertain whether any imports will be allowed into the country,” she said.\",\n  \"- e203 [rumour; straitstimes.com; original source The Information 2026-07-08; from gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c 2026-07-08] Chinese officials reportedly told Alibaba, ByteDance and DeepSeek they would be permitted to purchase some Nvidia H200 chips; the report was published July 8, 2026.\\n    Quote: Chinese officials have informed companies such as Alibaba Group, ByteDance and DeepSeek that they will have permission to purchase some of the processors, which are used to develop AI models, the news site said on July 8 .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e264, e266; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n  \"- e264 [rumour; technologyreview.com; original source The Information ; from gnews:50a84fa6b2f3eb33f29948a4 2026-07-09] The Information reported that China plans to permit Alibaba, ByteDance, and DeepSeek to buy Nvidia H200 chips.\\n    Quote: China plans to let its top AI firms buy Nvidia H200 chips\",\n  \"- e265 [fact; South China Morning Post; original source The U.S. government ; from gnews:91684b863d274b10246d0c13 2026-07-09] The U.S. approved H200 exports to China in early 2026.\\n    Quote: In early 2026, Washington took the rare step of approving Nvidia’s H200 graphics processing unit for export to China\",\n  \"- e266 [rumour; An unnamed source with knowledge of the matter, quoted by the South China Morning Post; original source An unnamed source with knowledge of the matter ; from gnews:91684b863d274b10246d0c13 2026-07-09] China plans to let selected companies, including Alibaba, buy limited numbers of Nvidia H200 chips, according to an unnamed source.\\n    Quote: Now, however, China’s stance is beginning to change, as the government plans to let selected companies – including Alibaba Group Holding – buy limited numbers of the device, a source with knowledge of the matter told the South China Morning Post.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e203; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n  \"- e284 [fact; moomoo.com; original source Chinese authorities ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] China is moving to grant limited approval for domestic firms to buy Nvidia H200 processors, with companies required to seek individual approval for quantities and use cases.\\n    Quote: The stock is also navigating a complex geopolitical environment as China moves to grant limited approval for domestic firms to purchase H200 processors. While this policy shift eases access to Nvidia’s technology, companies must now request individual approval for specific quantities and use cases.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e266 (); both are in force: China plans to let selected companies, including Alibaba, buy limited numbers of Nvidia H200 chips, according to an unnamed source.\",\n  \"- e359 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source Bloomberg ; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] A Bloomberg survey found Chinese technology companies expect domestic suppliers to receive roughly 46% of their AI chip spending over the next year, versus about 30% currently.\\n    Quote: A new Bloomberg survey showed Chinese technology companies expect to allocate roughly 46% of their AI chip spending to domestic suppliers over the next year, up from about 30% currently.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e225; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n  \"- e386 [party_statement; 247wallst.com, Alex Sirois; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:799ec454c15ddf61f08d042f 2026-07-13] NVIDIA’s Q2 guidance assumes zero Data Center compute revenue from China.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA leans on scale and CUDA gravity, but assumes zero China Data Center compute revenue in Q2 guidance\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e395; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n  \"- e395 [fact; 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:b68c019528300c6a1fcd3515 2026-07-13] NVIDIA shipped no H20 compute products to China in Q1 FY2027 and excluded Data Center compute revenue from China in its Q2 FY2027 guidance.\\n    Quote: There were no H20 compute products shipped to China in Q1 FY27, and the Q2 FY27 guidance excludes any Data Center compute revenue from China.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e386, e402; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n  \"- e265 [fact; South China Morning Post; original source The U.S. government ; from gnews:91684b863d274b10246d0c13 2026-07-09] The U.S. approved H200 exports to China in early 2026.\\n    Quote: In early 2026, Washington took the rare step of approving Nvidia’s H200 graphics processing unit for export to China\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e411; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n  \"- e410 [party_statement; Jeffery Kessler; original source Jeffery Kessler 2026-07-14; from gnews:9d898f2bf43ed52a98e1587a 2026-07-14] Under Secretary of Commerce for Industry and Security Jeffery Kessler said very few H200 and equivalent chips had shipped to China and Hong Kong under issued licenses.\\n    Quote: The bottom line is very few shipments against licenses for H200s and equivalents have taken place. It's a very small quantity of chips,\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e419; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e264 (); both are in force: The Information reported that China plans to permit Alibaba, ByteDance, and DeepSeek to buy Nvidia H200 chips.\",\n  \"- e411 [fact; CNBC; original source The U.S. government ; from gnews:9d898f2bf43ed52a98e1587a 2026-07-14] The U.S. government issued licenses for H200 chip exports earlier in 2026.\\n    Quote: Licenses for the chips, which some in the administration say can be used for military purposes, were issued earlier this year .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e265; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n  \"- e412 [party_statement; Jeffery Kessler; original source Jeffery Kessler 2026-07-14; from gnews:9d898f2bf43ed52a98e1587a 2026-07-14] Jeffery Kessler said the U.S. government reviews H200 export applicants case by case, requiring national-security criteria and compliance inspections.\\n    Quote: Kessler said that the U.S. government assessed companies that want the H200 chips on a case-by-case basis, with applicants needing to meet national security requirements and submit to inspections to make sure the chips are compliant.\",\n  \"- e413 [party_statement; Jeffery Kessler; original source Jeffery Kessler 2026-07-14; from gnews:9d898f2bf43ed52a98e1587a 2026-07-14] Jeffery Kessler said the U.S. government denies some H200 export-license applications.\\n    Quote: \\\"There are cases where we deny the license applications we receive,\\\" Kessler said.\",\n  \"- e419 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source A U.S. commerce official ; from gnews:c73b90b63a733c6ab712fcdd 2026-07-14] A U.S. commerce official told Congress that NVIDIA H200 chip shipments to China had begun, with very few chips shipped so far.\\n    Quote: while the number of H200 chips shipped to China so far was \\\"very few,\\\" the shipments have commenced\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e410; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n  \"- e433 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source The Chinese government ; from gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb 2026-07-14] Beijing reportedly continued to bar Chinese companies from buying NVIDIA H200 GPUs, relying instead on domestic semiconductor suppliers to address the shortfall.\\n    Quote: However, Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy these AI processors — instead, it’s banking on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to make up for the shortfall, but it’s apparently still not enough.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e203 (2026-07-08); both are in force: Chinese officials reportedly told Alibaba, ByteDance and DeepSeek they would be permitted to purchase some Nvidia H200 chips; the report was published July 8, 2026.\",\n  \"- e453 [fact; Reuters; original source U.S. authorities ; from gnews:24268bfaab6ef1d7876b188c 2026-07-14] U.S. authorities permitted ZTE Kangxun Telecom and Maginfra to purchase NVIDIA H200 chips.\\n    Quote: ZTE Kangxun Telecom and server maker Maginfra have been permitted to purchase Nvidia's H200 chips\",\n  \"- e454 [rumour; Reuters, citing two sources familiar with the matter; original source Some Chinese cloud firms, as reported by unnamed sources ; from gnews:24268bfaab6ef1d7876b188c 2026-07-14] Unnamed sources said some Chinese cloud firms recently told partners and clients they may soon be able to obtain NVIDIA H200 chips, indicating progress in Chinese authorities' import reviews.\\n    Quote: some Chinese cloud firms have recently told partners and clients they may soon be able to obtain H200 chips, the sources said, indicating some progress in import reviews by Chinese authorities.\"\n]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; from pathlib import Path\nfor n in [1,2,4,6,7,9]:\n for c in json.loads(Path(f\\\"controls/control-packet-{n}.json\\\").read_text()):\n  if c[\\\"control\\\"] in (13,15):\n   print(\\\"CONTROL\\\",c[\\\"control\\\"])\n   for e in c[\\\"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\\\"]:\n    if any(t in e.lower() for t in [\\\"trillion\\\",\\\"650 billion\\\",\\\"export\\\",\\\"december 2025\\\",\\\"2026\\\"]): print(e)'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; from pathlib import Path\nfor n in [1,2,4,6,7,9]:\n for c in json.loads(Path(f\\\"controls/control-packet-{n}.json\\\").read_text()):\n  if c[\\\"control\\\"] in (13,15):\n   print(\\\"CONTROL\\\",c[\\\"control\\\"])\n   for e in c[\\\"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\\\"]:\n    if any(t in e.lower() for t in [\\\"trillion\\\",\\\"650 billion\\\",\\\"export\\\",\\\"december 2025\\\",\\\"2026\\\"]): print(e)'\"","aggregated_output":"CONTROL 13\n- e48 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] NVIDIA projects that the four largest AI hyperscalers’ combined data-center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027.\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\n- e48 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] NVIDIA projects that the four largest AI hyperscalers’ combined data-center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027.\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e94; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e94 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f 2026-07-06] NVIDIA management forecast that hyperscaler spending would exceed $1 trillion in 2027.\n    Quote: During its last quarterly conference call, management forecast that it expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e48; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e94 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f 2026-07-06] NVIDIA management forecast that hyperscaler spending would exceed $1 trillion in 2027.\n    Quote: During its last quarterly conference call, management forecast that it expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e48, e185; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e185 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] NVIDIA claims the four largest AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027.\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e94, e193; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e193 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-07; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] The author estimated AI hyperscalers’ data-center capital expenditures could exceed $1 trillion in calendar 2027.\n    Quote: next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e185; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e185 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] NVIDIA claims the four largest AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027.\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e94, e193, e347; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e347 [party_statement; fool.com analyst; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:07cf5835d106467db5448c13 2026-07-12] NVIDIA told investors it expects AI hyperaccelerator spending to exceed $1 trillion next year; the item does not specify the spending populati