ng to grant limited approval for domestic firms to buy NVIDIA H200 processors, with firms required to seek individual approval for quantities and use cases.\n    Quote: The stock is also navigating a complex geopolitical environment as China moves to grant limited approval for domestic firms to purchase H200 processors. While this policy shift eases access to Nvidia’s technology, companies must now request individual approval for specific quantities and use cases.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e222; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e320 [third_party_view; David Moadel / 24/7 Wall St.; original source NVIDIA management (outlook assumption); David Moadel / 24/7 Wall St. (upside interpretation) ; from gnews:cbb9ff1a37ebb451f9321da5 2026-07-10] NVIDIA's Q2 FY2027 revenue outlook assumed zero Data Center compute revenue from China; the author says a policy change permitting H200 exports could add upside.\n    Quote: Notably, that outlook assumed zero Data Center compute revenue from China, meaning any policy thaw on H200 exports could represent additional upside.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e313; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e320 [third_party_view; David Moadel / 24/7 Wall St.; original source NVIDIA management (outlook assumption); David Moadel / 24/7 Wall St. (upside interpretation) ; from gnews:cbb9ff1a37ebb451f9321da5 2026-07-10] NVIDIA's Q2 FY2027 revenue outlook assumed zero Data Center compute revenue from China; the author says a policy change permitting H200 exports could add upside.\n    Quote: Notably, that outlook assumed zero Data Center compute revenue from China, meaning any policy thaw on H200 exports could represent additional upside.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e313, e410; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e430 [party_statement; Jeffery Kessler; original source Jeffery Kes